const ANSWER_KEY = {"1": "D", "2": "D", "3": "C", "4": "B", "5": "B", "6": "C", "7": "B", "8": "B", "9": "E", "10": "D", "11": "C", "12": "F", "13": "A", "14": "F", "15": "C", "16": "D", "17": "C", "18": "C", "19": "C", "20": "B", "21": "B", "22": "A", "23": "B", "24": "C", "25": "C", "26": "E", "27": "C", "28": "B", "29": "D", "30": "B", "31": "C", "32": "C", "33": "C", "34": "D", "35": "D", "36": "B", "37": "C", "38": "A", "39": "C", "40": "A", "41": "D", "42": "E", "43": "A", "44": "E", "45": "A", "46": "A", "47": "H", "48": "C", "49": "C", "50": "E"};
const EXPLANATIONS = {"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In normal development, adolescents form their own identity and may separate from their parents in the process. This can manifest as forming their own opinions and breaking rules.</p><p>This patient is demonstrating behaviors reflective of normal development. It would be appropriate for the physician to provide parental education about adolescent development and reassurance that the patient's current behavior is normal. According to the Erikson stages of psychosocial development, a key developmental task for adolescents is to formulate their identity as an independent person. This task involves recognizing that their parents possess their own flawed identities and separating from their parents by expressing their own opinions and preferences. A degree of teenage rebellion against authority is normal (eg, not complying with bedtime, questioning family rules).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Family therapy (Choice A) is indicated for oppositional defiant disorder or conduct disorder. This patient's defiance is not excessive or impairing and is therefore consistent with normal adolescent behavior. Individual psychotherapy (Choice B) is indicated for many psychiatric disorders, including depression and anxiety disorder. This patient demonstrates normal adolescent behavior, so individual psychotherapy is not indicated. Measurement of serum thyrotropin-releasing hormone concentration (Choice C) helps distinguish causes of thyroid dysfunction. This patient does not have vital sign or physical examination abnormalities (eg, hair or skin changes) consistent with thyroid dysfunction. Polysomnography (Choice E) helps diagnose causes of unrestful sleep such as sleep apnea. This patient is tired in the morning because she stays up late reading. Reinstitute methylphenidate therapy (Choice F) would be indicated for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. This patient does not demonstrate significant inattentive, hyperactive, or impulsive symptoms. Her rebellion is volitional, not impulsive, and reflects the normal developmental stage of questioning rules.</p>", "2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Obsessive-compulsive disorder manifests as recurrent, intrusive urges or thoughts (obsessions) that patients commonly try to relieve or neutralize by repetitive rituals (compulsions). Many patients demonstrate both obsessions and compulsions, though some patients experience either obsessions or compulsions only. Obsessions about harm to others are common.</p><p>Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) manifests as recurrent, intrusive, anxiety-inducing urges or thoughts (obsessions) that the patient typically tries to relieve or neutralize by repetitive rituals (compulsions). These symptoms cause significant distress and/or daily dysfunction. Common obsessions are related to cleanliness, symmetry, harm to self or others (as in this patient), and taboo thoughts or ideas. Compulsions are rigidly applied repetitive behaviors (eg, checking, washing) or mental activities (eg, counting, repeating words) that relieve anxiety related to obsessions. Some patients demonstrate both obsessions and compulsions, though other patients experience either obsessions or compulsions only. OCD typically presents relatively early in life. It is commonly treated with cognitive behavioral therapy and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors such as paroxetine. Clomipramine and venlafaxine are second-line medication options.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Buspirone (Choice A) is a serotonin modulator useful for generalized anxiety disorder. It is not helpful for OCD. Cyproheptadine (Choice B) is a serotonin antagonist used to treat serotonin syndrome. A serotonin antagonist is unlikely to help anxiety disorder symptoms such as Lorazepam (Choice C) is unlikely to be helpful. Benzodiazepines have no proven efficacy for OCD. Risperidone (Choice E) has a small amount of evidence for refractory OCD. However, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors are the mainstay of treatment.</p>", "3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: An acute dystonic reaction most commonly occurs in response to potent dopamine-2 (D2) antagonists such as typical antipsychotics and presents with torticollis, retrocollis, opisthotonos, and/or oculogyric crisis. Treatment involves anticholinergic medications, such as diphenhydramine or benztropine, which restore the dopaminergic-cholinergic balance in the basal ganglia.</p><p>Acute dystonic reactions are involuntary, painful muscle spasms that occur most frequently in response to potent dopamine-2 (D2) antagonists such as typical antipsychotics (eg, haloperidol). It is postulated that an imbalance of dopamine (a movement facilitator) and acetylcholine (a movement inhibitor) in the nigrostriatal pathways contributes to acute dystonic reaction. Common presentations include torticollis (head tilted to the side and down), retrocollis (head tilted back), opisthotonos (arching of the back), and oculogyric crisis (involuntary deviation of the eyes). Patients rarely present with laryngospasm and stridor. The underlying pathophysiology is an imbalance in the dopaminergic-cholinergic pathways of the basal ganglia. Risk factors for development include male sex, young age, recent cocaine use, and a history of previous acute dystonic reaction. Acute dystonic reactions are treated with diphenhydramine or benztropine, which are both anticholinergic medications that work to restore the proper balance in the dopaminergic and cholinergic pathways.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Acetylcholine blockade (Choice A), such as with benztropine or diphenhydramine therapy, treats acute dystonic reaction. Acetylcholine blockade is not associated with causing acute dystonic reaction. Acetylcholine excess (Choice B) is not associated with acute dystonic reaction. It is thought that an imbalance of dopamine and acetylcholine contributes to acute dystonic reaction, though dopamine antagonism much more commonly leads to acute dystonic reaction. Dopamine excess (Choice D) in the nigrostriatal pathway is associated with excess movement (eg, chorea), not acute dystonic reaction. Serotonin blockade (Choice E) is associated with antiemetic effects. Serotonin excess (Choice F) is associated with serotonin syndrome. Serotonin is not commonly involved in movement regulation.</p>", "4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Adjustment disorder refers to the onset of disproportionate distress within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. Patients with adjustment disorder may experience associated depression or anxiety symptoms but do not meet the diagnostic criteria of depression or anxiety disorders.</p><p>Adjustment disorder refers to the onset of an emotional disturbance within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. The emotional disturbance manifests as marked distress out of proportion to the stressor and a significant impairment in daily school, work, or social functioning. Adjustment disorders are typically associated with anxiety, depression, or behavioral symptoms without meeting the criteria of anxiety or depressive disorders. For example, this patient demonstrates a sad mood and insomnia but no anhedonia or changes in energy or appetite. As adjustment disorder is typically self-limited, the treatment of adjustment disorder commonly includes brief supportive psychotherapy but may also include symptomatic treatment of anxiety or depressive symptoms (eg, sleep medication).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Acute stress disorder (Choice A) refers to intrusive thoughts, hyperarousal, avoidance, and/or negative mood/thoughts that occur within 1 month of a traumatic event such as a life-threatening motor vehicle collision. The traumatic event is defined as serious physical or sexual violence or trauma. A separation would not be classified as a traumatic event in this context. Bereavement (Choice C), or uncomplicated grief reaction, refers to a person's emotional response after a loved one dies and can include sadness, shock, anguish, guilt, regret, difficulty concentrating, decreased interest in usual activities, preoccupation with thoughts and memories of the deceased, and neurovegetative symptoms such as sleep, appetite, and energy disturbances. This patient did not experience the death of a loved one. Conversion disorder (Choice D), also referred to as functional neurological symptom disorder, can result in loss of motor or sensory function that cannot be explained by a physical illness or injury. This patient does not have neurologic symptoms or show neurologic abnormalities on physical examination. Major depressive disorder (Choice E) includes 2 or more weeks of five of the following symptoms: depressed mood, anhedonia (eg, decreased interest in socializing), guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). This patient does not have enough symptoms to meet major depressive disorder criteria.</p>", "5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder that features acute episodes of mania and may include episodes of depression. Acute mania typically manifests with an irritable or euphoric mood, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility, and grandiose thinking. Some patients demonstrate associated psychotic symptoms (eg, erotomanic delusions as in this patient, hallucinations).</p><p>This patient is experiencing an episode of acute mania and can therefore be diagnosed with bipolar disorder. Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder that features acute episodes of mania and may include episodes of depression. Acute mania typically manifests with an irritable and/or euphoric mood, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility (as evidenced by this patient's overspending), and grandiose thinking. Some patients demonstrate associated psychotic symptoms (eg, erotomanic delusions as in this patient, hallucinations). Acute mania can severely disrupt patients’ daily function and endanger their safety. Management of acute mania typically requires mood stabilizing therapy (eg, mood stabilizers or mood stabilizing atypical antipsychotics). S is patient is experiencing an episode of acute mania and can therefore be diagnosed with bipolar disorder. Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder that features acute ~ episodes of mania and may include episodes of depression. Acute mania typically manifests with an irritable and/or euphoric mood, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility (as evidenced by this patient's overspending), and grandiose thinking. Some patients demonstrate associated psychotic symptoms (eg, erotomanic delusions as in this patient, hallucinations). Acute mania can severely disrupt patients’ daily function and endanger their safety. Management of acute mania typically requires mood stabilizing therapy (eg, mood stabilizers or mood stabilizing atypical antipsychotics).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Acute stress disorder (Choice A) refers to intrusive thoughts, hyperarousal, avoidance, and/or negative mood/thoughts that occur within 1 month of a life-threatening traumatic event. This patient has not experienced a traumatic event recently, and her symptoms are more consistent with mania than acute stress disorder. Dissociative identity disorder (Choice C), formerly known as multiple personality disorder, refers to patients transitioning between several identities, sometimes within the same day, and is frequently related to childhood trauma. This patient's behavior change is more prolonged and persistent than identity changes of dissociative identity disorder. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (Choice D) is an anxiety disorder typically characterized by obsessions (unwanted, intrusive thoughts that produce anxiety) that the patient attempts to neutralize with compulsions (repetitive behaviors). This patient demonstrates an erotomanic delusion, or a fixed, false belief, even in the face of compelling evidence otherwise. Patients with obsessive-compulsive disorder demonstrate more cognitive flexibility in that they commonly believe evidence of their obsessive thoughts being false. As well, decreased need for sleep and irresponsible behaviors are more consistent with acute mania. Schizophrenia (Choice E) presents with at least two of the following five symptoms: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, or negative symptoms (eg, flat affect, apathy, alogia). This patient does not demonstrate negative symptoms, instead presenting with acute manic symptoms (eg, irritability, decreased need for sleep) consistent with bipolar disorder.</p>", "6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Alcohol use disorder and associated malnutrition may result in vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency, which can cause Wernicke encephalopathy. Wernicke encephalopathy is characterized by altered mental status, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, and ataxia. Patients with Wernicke encephalopathy should be treated with intravenous thiamine to prevent progression to Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, a chronic memory disorder with anterograde and retrograde amnesia, along with the consequent tendency to confabulate.</p><p>Patients with alcohol use disorder typically have a significant vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency due to poor nutritional intake, thiamine malabsorption, and impaired cellular utilization of thiamine. Over time, thiamine deficiency can result in damage and atrophy of the mammillary bodies, visible on MRI of the brain, which manifests as Wernicke encephalopathy. The typical clinical presentation of Wernicke encephalopathy is a triad of acutely altered mental status, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, and ataxia. Patients may present with physical examination findings and laboratory studies consistent with liver cirrhosis, such as gynecomastia and palmar erythema. The symptoms of Wernicke encephalopathy are reversible but increase the risk for developing Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome (also known as Korsakoff psychosis) in patients with alcohol use disorder if not immediately treated with intravenous thiamine. Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is an irreversible condition that features anterograde and retrograde amnesia along with the consequent tendency to confabulate, or verbalize false stories or memories, to compensate for the inability to remember.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Deficiency of folic acid (Choice A), or vitamin B9, is relatively common in patients with alcohol use disorder but does not typically lead to altered mental status or neurologic dysfunction. Folic acid deficiency more commonly manifests as macrocytic anemia and hypersegmented neutrophils with normal concentrations of methylmalonic acid. Niacin deficiency (Choice B) results in pellagra, which classically presents with the triad of dementia, diarrhea, and dermatitis. This patient does not have diarrhea or dermatitis. Vitamin B6 deficiency (Choice D) typically presents with microcytic anemia, dermatitis, glossitis, cheilosis, irritability, confusion, and depression. Ataxia, ophthalmoplegia, and nystagmus would be atypical. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency (Choice E) may occur in patients with alcohol use disorder but typically leads to subacute combined degeneration, which usually presents with ataxia, weakness, and numbness (due to demyelination of the spinocerebellar tracts, lateral corticospinal tracts, and dorsal columns) as well as macrocytic anemia, hypersegmented neutrophils, and increased methylmalonic acid. Ophthalmoplegia and altered mental status would be atypical.</p>", "7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: When a patient has several acute risk factors for suicide and/or homicide, hospitalization should be considered. In the absence of homicidal or suicidal ideation, outpatient safety planning can be pursued. Restricted access to lethal means, especially firearms, is a crucial part of this safety planning.</p><p>This patient has several acute risk factors for suicide and homicide, indicating that his access to lethal means should be restricted. His acute risk factors for homicide include paranoid ideation, increased impulsivity, and possession of a firearm. It is also concerning that the patient is demonstrating a preoccupation with violence, as evidenced by his song choices. His acute risk factors for suicide include increased impulsivity and possession of a firearm. Given that the patient has multiple acute tisk factors for suicide and homicide, psychiatric hospitalization should be considered. However, given that the patient has not threatened violence and denies current suicidal or homicidal ideation, it is reasonable to create an outpatient safety plan. An important element of a safety plan is restricting the patient's access to lethal means such as firearms. The physician should attempt to obtain buy-in from the patient to dispose of or restrict his access to his firearm. After attending to the immediate safety risk, antipsychotic medication should be started if the patient is agreeable, and the cause of this patient's psychotic symptoms should be elucidated.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Administering aripiprazole (Choice A) may begin to address the patient's psychosis within days to weeks. However, the patient's access to a firearm poses the most immediate risk. Have the patient sign a contract for safety (Choice C) refers to agreements that a patient signs promising to abstain from violence. Safety contracts have not proven efficacious in preventing violence. Notifying the patient's parents and the rock band about his potential for danger (Choice D) is not needed at this time. The patient has not expressed homicidal ideation or specific threats toward his parents or the rock band. Reporting the patient's behavior to child protective services (Choice E) does not address the immediate safety risk of the patient's firearm access. It is also likely to be unnecessary at this time since the patient has not expressed paranoia or threats toward his children.</p>", "8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is an anxiety disorder typically characterized by obsessions (unwanted, intrusive thoughts that produce anxiety) that the patient attempts to neutralize with compulsions (repetitive behaviors). OCD is commonly treated with cognitive behavioral therapy and/or selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor medications.</p><p>Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) manifests as recurrent, intrusive urges or thoughts (obsessions) that the patient tries to relieve or neutralize by repetitive rituals (compulsions). Common obsessions are related to cleanliness, symmetry, harm to self or others, and taboo thoughts or ideas. Compulsions are rigidly applied repetitive behaviors (eg, checking, washing) or mental activities (eg, counting, repeating words) that relieve anxiety related to obsessions. These symptoms are time- consuming and cause significant anxiety to the patient and markedly impair functioning (eg, lead to lateness issues for this patient). Patients with OCD also commonly experience anxiety or major mood disorders. OCD typically presents relatively early in life, with most cases present by age 20 years. It is commonly treated with cognitive behavioral therapy as well as selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor medications. Clomipramine and venlafaxine are second-line medication options.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Generalized anxiety disorder (Choice A) is an anxiety disorder that features excessive worrying about a range of topics with psychological and physical symptoms and associated functional impairment. This patient's repetitive, intrusive thoughts about harm to others are more consistent with an obsession. Panic disorder (Choice C) is an anxiety disorder characterized by recurrent panic attacks that are unexpected and associated with worry about future panic attacks or avoidance of panic attack triggers. This patient's anxiety is not unexpected; it is predictably triggered by staircases and driving. Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) (Choice D) refers to an excessive fear of scrutiny, embarrassment, or rejection in social and/or performance situations, leading to significant distress and/or impaired functioning. Though this patient prefers his parents’ company, there is no clear evidence of a pervasive fear of social rejection. Instead, this patient's anxiety centers around harming others. Specific phobia (Choice E) is an anxiety disorder featuring anxiety about a certain situation or object that commonly leads to avoidance behavior. This patient's repetitive checking is consistent with a compulsion. As well, this patient's anxiety is triggered by an obsessive idea (the idea that he might harm others) rather than a physical situation.</p>", "9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A sense of invulnerability, arising from the beginnings of the formal operational stage, is common in adolescents. Piaget's stages of cognitive development describe the ability of children to use thinking and learning to navigate the world.</p><p>This patient demonstrates a sense of invulnerability, consistent with the principle of adolescent egocentrism. According to Jean Piaget's stages of cognitive development, adolescents enter the formal operational stage and begin thinking abstractly (eg, using logic rather than relying on direct observation). When they enter this stage, they experience their thoughts as powerful and begin to direct their thoughts inward toward themselves (egocentrism). Since adolescents still have difficulty differentiating their own thoughts from others’ thoughts, they presume that others are focused on them as well, causing them to believe their experiences are unique and special. This sense of uniqueness is hypothesized to contribute to a sense of invulnerability to risks such as consequences of unprotected sex or death. Research studies on the validity of this theory of adolescent invulnerability have been mixed. Piaget's stages of cognitive development describe the ability of children to use thinking and learning to navigate the world. The stages progress over time across the sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational, and formal operational stages.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Abstract thinking (Choice A) is beginning to develop in adolescents. However, the lack of fully developed abstract thinking deters these patients from considering the similarities and differences between their thoughts and others’ thoughts. As described above, this lack of abstract thought leads to an inability to fully appreciate risks. Concrete thinking (Choice B) refers to reasoning based on perceptual information about the physical world. This patient demonstrates a degree of abstract thinking in that she can consider the hypothetical situation of pregnancy. Delusional thinking (Choice C) involves fixed false beliefs. This patient does not convey complete certainty in her belief that she will not become pregnant. Mature sexuality (Choice D) is not a widely adopted concept. It is thought that there is not one universal benchmark indicating sexual maturity. However, this patient demonstrates immaturity in her cognitive approach to sex.</p>", "10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Opioid intoxication causes symptoms of central nervous system (CNS) depression including sedation, respiratory depression, bradycardia, and hypotension. Miosis differentiates opioid intoxication from other CNS depressant toxidromes.</p><p>This patient is likely intoxicated with heroin, an opioid drug. Opioids, which are central nervous system (CNS) depressants used as analgesics and recreational drugs, act throughout the CNS and peripheral nervous system and interact with several neurotransmitter systems, leading to diverse effects. Opioid intoxication causes euphoria due to interaction with dopamine, altered mental status, sedation, bradycardia and hypotension, depressed respiratory drive, and constricted pupils. Miosis is a distinctive finding that is less common in intoxication with other CNS depressants and is caused by direct opioid receptor activity in brain areas responsible for pupillary control. Opioids also act on receptors within the enteric nervous system, decreasing gut motility and thus causing constipation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Alcohol (Choice A) and clonazepam (Choice B) are CNS depressants that can cause slurred speech, ataxia, emotional lability, and memory lapses. In severe intoxication, stupor and respiratory depression can also occur. This patient's miosis makes the presentation more likely due to opioid intoxication. Cocaine (Choice C) is an addictive recreational drug that typically causes euphoria, restlessness, and increased sympathetic tone (tachycardia, hypertension, pupillary dilation). This patient has miosis, not mydriasis, making cocaine less likely. LSD (Choice E) is a potent hallucinogen known to cause auditory and visual hallucinations, depersonalization, flashbacks, and paranoia. PCP (phencyclidine) (Choice F) can cause similar symptoms to LSD and is also associated with aggression, rotatory nystagmus, and ataxia. This patient has no nystagmus, making PCP intoxication less likely.</p>", "11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Delirium presents with acute disturbances in awareness, attention, and baseline cognition that fluctuate in severity during the course of the day and may also present with perceptual disturbances such as paranoid delusions. Meperidine is one of the most deliriogenic opioids.</p><p>Delirium is an acute confusional state typically resulting from acute medical illness, sleep disturbances, substance use, and/or medications. Common medications associated with delirium include anticholinergics, opioids, and benzodiazepines. Of opioid medications, meperidine and tramadol have been found to be highly deliriogenic. Delirium presents with acute disturbances in awareness, attention, and baseline cognition that fluctuate in severity during the course of the day. Patients may present with perceptual disturbances (eg, delusions as in this patient, hallucinations). Management of medication-induced delirium should involve discontinuing the offending medication, optimizing exacerbating medical conditions, frequent reorientation, maintenance of normal sleep-wake cycles, and placement of the patient in a calm and soothing environment.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Chloroquine (Choice A) is an antimalarial agent used to treat systemic lupus erythematosus. Hydrochlorothiazide (Choice B) is an antihypertensive agent. Propranolol (Choice D) is a beta blocker used to treat tremor, performance anxiety, and hypertension. Thyroxine (Choice E) is used to treat hypothyroidism. None of these medications are associated with delirium (ie, none are found on the Beers Criteria medication list).</p>", "12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In somatic symptom disorder, the patient is excessively preoccupied with one or more somatic symptoms such that these symptoms disrupt daily life. These symptoms may or may not originate from an underlying disease though are typically unconsciously produced or exacerbated by psychological factors such as stress.</p><p>Somatic symptom disorder is the most likely cause for this patient's pain symptoms in the setting of ongoing symptoms despite extensive evaluation and may be diagnosed on a psychiatric assessment. According to the DSM-5, patients with somatic symptom disorder are preoccupied with an existing somatic symptom such that these symptoms disrupt the patient's daily life. Somatic symptoms are thought to represent the unconscious conversion of psychological distress to somatic symptoms. As such, a trauma history is common. These symptoms may or may not originate from underlying disease (eg, tissue damage) and may or may not be accompanied by mood or anxiety disorder diagnoses. Patients with somatic symptom disorder persistently devote excessive time and energy to these symptoms or related health concerns (eg, repeatedly going to the doctor). Somatic symptom disorder typically demonstrates a chronic and fluctuating course. Management of somatic symptom disorder centers around regular primary care follow-up that targets coping skills, provides reassurance, and avoids unnecessary tests.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Functional neurologic symptom disorder (Choice A) involves neurologic symptoms such as sensory or motor dysfunction that are not fully explained by objective findings on physical examination or imaging. Common examples include blindness, aphasia, and unexplained weakness. Functional neurologic symptom disorder is postulated to represent the subconscious conversion of intolerable emotional distress into neurologic symptoms. Histrionic personality disorder (Choice B), a cluster B personality disorder, is characterized by theatrical, superficial expressions of emotion that unconsciously serve to garner attention from others to fulfill emotional needs. Patients may behave seductively. This patient's attempts to gain the physician's attention center around assuaging her pain, not seduction. postulated to represent the subconscious conversion of intolerable emotional distress into neurologic symptoms. Histrionic personality disorder (Choice B), a cluster B personality disorder, is characterized by theatrical, superficial expressions of emotion that unconsciously serve to garner attention from others to fulfill emotional needs. Patients may behave seductively. This patient's attempts to gain the physician's attention center around assuaging her pain, not seduction. IlIness anxiety disorder (Choice C) is characterized by excessive anxiety and preoccupation about the possibility of having or acquiring a serious illness such that patients perform frequent health-related behaviors (eg, repeatedly going to the doctor) or exhibit maladaptive avoidance (eg, completely avoiding going to the doctor), which persists for at least six months. This patient is preoccupied with her existing pain symptoms, not the possibility that she may have a serious illness. Major depressive disorder (Choice D) includes two or more weeks of at least five of the following symptoms: depressed mood, anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor retardation, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). This patient does not report depressive symptoms. Psychological factors affecting medical condition (Choice E) refers to psychological or behavioral factors that exacerbate a medical condition or interfere with its treatment. Though psychological factors contribute to the generation of her somatic symptoms, no medical condition (eg, disease or tissue damage) has been identified.</p>", "13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Normal sleep changes with age include a slightly decreased total sleep duration, slightly increased sleep latency, decreased proportion of deep sleep and REM sleep, and increased nighttime awakenings. Reassurance is sufficient for patients with normal age-related sleep changes.</p><p>Older adults typically experience several sleep-related changes as they age. A slightly decreased total sleep duration (eg, 6.5 to 7 hours), slightly increased sleep latency, increased nighttime awakenings (three per night is normal), and a decreased proportion of time spent in the deep sleep and REM sleep stages are typical. The increase in nighttime awakenings can be attributed to less time spent in deep sleep and nocturia (a result of age-related changes in the urinary system). Management should include reassurance, and counseling on sleep hygiene can help patients optimize their sleep patterns.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Recommending staying up later to avoid getting up so early (Choice B) would be unnecessary in this patient with normal sleep changes. In patients with primary insomnia, staying up until sleepiness occurs and waking up at the same time each morning are common strategies. Recommending warm milk before bedtime (Choice C) may be appropriately suggested later, as positive expectancy along with tryptophan in milk may modestly increase sleepiness. However, this patient who is anxious about his sleep patterns should first be reassured that the sleep changes are normal. Recommending diphenhydramine (Choice D) or prescribing temazepam (Choice E) would be unnecessary given this patient's normal sleep patterns. As well, antihistamine and benzodiazepine medications should be used with caution in elderly patients because these medications put elderly patients at risk for delirium, oversedation, and falls.</p>", "14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) refers to an excessive fear of scrutiny or embarrassment in social and/or performance situations that commonly leads to avoidance behavior. Treatment typically involves cognitive behavioral therapy and antidepressant medication.</p><p>Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) refers to an excessive fear of scrutiny, embarrassment, or rejection in social and/or performance situations, leading to significant distress and/or impaired functioning. Patients with social anxiety disorder commonly exhibit shyness and performance anxiety. Performance anxiety refers to the fear of negative evaluation in situations such as public speaking, test taking, and sex. Patients’ fears about performance may extend to other public rituals such as eating. Patients with social anxiety disorder may experience decreased anxiety when interacting in more intimate settings or with familiar people compared with socializing in groups or with strangers. Body-focused behaviors (eg, nail-biting) are common in patients with anxiety disorders. Treatment typically involves cognitive behavioral therapy and antidepressant medication (eg, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors) and may also include as-needed anxiolytic medication. significant distress and/or impaired functioning. Patients with social anxiety disorder commonly exhibit shyness and performance anxiety. Performance anxiety refers ~ to the fear of negative evaluation in situations such as public speaking, test taking, and sex. Patients’ fears about performance may extend to other public rituals such as eating. Patients with social anxiety disorder may experience decreased anxiety when interacting in more intimate settings or with familiar people compared with socializing in groups or with strangers. Body-focused behaviors (eg, nail-biting) are common in patients with anxiety disorders. Treatment typically involves cognitive behavioral therapy and antidepressant medication (eg, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors) and may also include as-needed anxiolytic medication.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Delusional disorder (Choice A) is a psychotic disorder that features the presence of one or more delusions for 1 month or longer with no other psychotic symptoms. This patient demonstrates anxiety about the possibility of social rejection rather than a fixed, false belief. Generalized anxiety disorder (Choice B) is an anxiety disorder that features excessive worrying about a range of topics with psychological and physical symptoms and associated functional impairment. This patient's anxiety is specific to social situations. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (Choice C) is an anxiety disorder typically characterized by obsessions (unwanted, intrusive thoughts that produce anxiety) that the patient attempts to neutralize with compulsions (repetitive behaviors). This patient does not demonstrate repetitive behaviors that he feels he must perform to neutralize his anxiety. Agoraphobia (Choice D) refers to the fear of leaving one’s home. Though the patient reports fears of being negatively evaluated for public behaviors, he does not specifically fear leaving his home. Paranoid personality disorder (Choice E) is a cluster A personality disorder, the odd or eccentric cluster, characterized by a pervasive mistrust of others and viewing others as possessing malicious intent. Though the patient fears others will negatively evaluate him, he does not fear others harming him.</p>", "15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Domestic abuse should be suspected in patients who present with frequent injuries with inconsistent explanations and/or a suspicious distribution of bruises on the lower abdomen, back, or forearms. Pregnant women, children, and the elderly are at a particularly high risk for abuse, although anyone may be a victim. Patients who are unwilling or unable to report their abuser should be encouraged to develop a safety plan should the need to flee arise.</p><p>Domestic abuse or intimate partner violence (IPV) is the most likely explanation for this patient's distribution of bruising and frequent injuries requiring emergency visits. IPV should be considered in patients with a suspicious pattern of bruising and/or explanations for the mechanism of injury that are inconsistent or do not match physical examination findings. Areas of bruising that are particularly worrying include the lower abdomen, posterior rib cage, and head. The forearms may become bruised while employing defensive movements to prevent injury to the face or torso during an attack. Risk factors for IPV include recent job loss or job/financial instability and substance use. IPV often increases or begins during pregnancy, especially unintended pregnancy. All patients with physical examination findings suspicious for IPV should be asked in a tactful and nonthreatening manner if they feel safe at home. If possible, all patients should be assessed alone to prevent intimidation that may occur when the partner is present. If patients express that they are being abused, it is the provider's role to assess whether they are prepared to act. Often, patients are not yet willing or able to leave a violent relationship and understanding this requires empathy on the part of the provider. However, all patients should receive assistance with developing a safety plan, which includes identifying a place to flee if necessary, developing and relaying signals or code words to children or neighbors who can call for help, and preparing an emergency kit stored outside of the home with necessary items that might be required if the patient needs to flee. Written materials such as pamphlets should generally be avoided as these can be discovered by the abuser. Many states require mandatory reporting for the abuse of children, elderly, and disabled persons, but requirements for reporting the abuse of adults varies by state.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Admitting the patient to an inpatient psychiatric facility (Choice A) is incorrect. While this patient is in danger of harm, this is as a result of her domestic situation, rather than a psychiatric condition. Admission to an inpatient facility would only temporarily remove her from this situation rather than providing her with assistance to Contacting the police (Choice B) may be required in some states where adult abuse is included in mandatory reporting, but it should not be the first step in addressing IPV. Involving law enforcement or recommending the patient file an order of protection from her husband (Choice E) before the patient is willing and able to safely leave may only escalate the situation and increase the risk for harm. Recommending anger management therapy to the patient's husband (Choice D) is incorrect. Interventions for perpetrators of IPV are often minimally successful, and therapy has not conclusively been shown to decrease the risk for violence.</p>", "16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: By antagonizing dopamine receptors in the tuberoinfundibular pathway, risperidone leads to hyperprolactinemia and decreased testosterone concentrations in males. Male patients on risperidone therapy can present with gynecomastia, galactorrhea, erectile dysfunction, and/or decreased libido.</p><p>Risperidone is an atypical antipsychotic medication, used occasionally in refractory obsessive-compulsive disorder, that acts as a potent D2 dopamine antagonist. It antagonizes dopamine in the mesolimbic, nigrostriatal, and tuberoinfundibular pathways, corresponding to improvement of psychotic symptoms, worsening of extrapyramidal symptoms, and sexual dysfunction, respectively. In the tuberoinfundibular pathway, dopamine antagonism leads to hyperprolactinemia, manifesting as gynecomastia (as in this patient) and/or galactorrhea. Of the antipsychotic medications, risperidone is especially well known for causing hyperprolactinemia. Prolactin suppresses gonadotropin-releasing hormone, which decreases release of luteinizing hormone and follicle-stimulating hormone in females, leading to gonadal dysfunction. In males, decreased testicular release of testosterone leads to erectile dysfunction and decreased libido. In females, decreased ovarian release of estrogen is associated with amenorrhea and infertility. If antipsychotic-induced hyperprolactinemia is asymptomatic, then no changes are needed. In the case of symptomatic hyperprolactinemia, as in this patient, options include discontinuing risperidone, adding a dopamine agonist or partial agonist (eg, aripiprazole), or adding testosterone.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Alcohol consumption (Choice A), if chronic and heavy, can lead to decreased libido and impaired vasodilation of the vasculature of the corpora cavernosa, resulting in erectile dysfunction. However, this patient's alcohol consumption is moderate (ie, within the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention’s recommendation of fewer than 14 drinks weekly for males). Diabetic neuropathy (Choice B) typically develops after several years of poorly controlled diabetes mellitus. As well, gynecomastia would be uncommon. Metformin therapy (Choice C) can occasionally lead to decreased testosterone and, consequently, decreased libido and erectile dysfunction. However, gynecomastia, which is typically mediated by hyperprolactinemia, would be uncommon. Sertraline therapy (Choice E) can commonly cause sexual dysfunction within all three phases of sexual response (libido, sexual arousal, and orgasm) through its serotonergic effects. However, gynecomastia, which is typically mediated by hyperprolactinemia, would be uncommon.</p>", "17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is characterized by excessive daytime sleepiness and snoring. Congenital anatomic abnormalities of the oropharynx and upper airway, as in the mucopolysaccharidoses, are a rare cause of OSA. Diagnosis involves polysomnography. Treatment commonly involves enzyme replacement therapy or surgical correction.</p><p>Polysomnography is used to diagnose obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), which is likely in this patient. Headaches that occur in the mornings are frequently reported by patients with untreated OSA. This patient is likely experiencing sleep-related hypoventilation due to OSA. Clinical features of untreated OSA include excessive daytime fatigue, falling asleep at inappropriate times, loud snoring, and a disrupted sleep-wake cycle. Risk factors include male sex, obesity, congenital or traumatic anatomic abnormalities of the oropharynx and upper airway, neuromuscular disorders, and use of sedating medications. Untreated OSA is associated with hypertension. Management commonly involves adenotonsillar surgery for cases with obstructing lesions of the airway, though sleep apnea secondary to mucopolysaccharidosis IIl can also respond well to enzyme replacement therapy. Mucopolysaccharidoses are a type of lysosomal storage disorder caused by an enzyme deficiency that prevents the degradation of glycosaminoglycans in the lysosome, leading to cellular dysfunction. Patients with mucopolysaccharidosis III typically demonstrate progressive central nervous system degeneration (eg, developmental delay, behavioral dysregulation), musculoskeletal abnormalities (eg, coarse facial features, joint contractures), and hepatosplenomegaly. Sleep disorders such as sleep apnea are common.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Complete blood count (Choice A) is unlikely to be helpful in diagnosing this patient's sleep episodes. Mucopolysaccharidoses are not associated with hematologic complications, and anemia typically presents with fatigue, not sleepiness. EEG (Choice B) may assist in diagnosing seizures. However, this patient does not demonstrate evidence of seizure-like events or postictal confusion. The patient's incidents of sleeping at his desk are likely secondary to daytime sleepiness from OSA. CT scan of the head (Choice D) and lumbar puncture (Choice E) are helpful in diagnosing hydrocephalus, a complication of blockage of arachnoid granulations by cellular material in mucopolysaccharidoses. Patients may present with a morning headache and other signs of increased intracranial pressure such as papilledema, visual disturbances, and progressive neurologic dysfunction. Other than morning headaches, this patient's presentation is not consistent with hydrocephalus.</p>", "18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Symptoms of major depressive disorder include 2 or more weeks of depressed mood, anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor slowing, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). Prominent mental status examination findings commonly include a depressed affect with a decreased affective range, poor eye contact, response latency (consistent with psychomotor slowing), and distractibility.</p><p>This patient meets criteria for major depressive disorder (MDD). MDD is characterized by 2 or more weeks of at least five of the following symptoms: depressed mood, anhedonia (eg, decreased hedonic value of food), guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor slowing, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). These symptoms disrupt everyday functioning. Prominent mental status examination findings commonly include a depressed affect with a decreased affective range, poor eye contact, response latency (consistent with psychomotor slowing), and distractibility. Mild abnormalities on cognitive testing may be present, particularly in older patients. This patient should be carefully assessed for suicidal ideation, as suicide completion rates are especially high in older men. The most effective treatment includes both psychotherapy and antidepressant medication.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Adjustment disorder with depressed mood (Choice A) refers to the onset of disproportionate distress within 3 months of an identifiable stressor with associated depressive symptoms that do not meet the diagnostic criteria for MDD. This patient meets the diagnostic criteria of MDD. Dysthymic disorder (Choice B), also known as persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia), refers to depressive symptoms lasting longer than 2 years. By definition, patients with dysthymic disorder do not meet MDD criteria. Mood disorder secondary to a general medical condition (Choice D) is unlikely in this patient. Physiologic tremors (an action tremor) and mild pedal edema are relatively common in older adults and typically do not suggest a serious medical illness. Normal aging (Choice E) includes a slow decline of executive functioning, processing speed, episodic and working memory, attention span, and problem solving. MDD is not a part of normal aging.</p>", "19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Delusional disorder is a psychotic disorder that features the presence of one or more delusions for 1 month or longer with no other psychotic or mood symptoms. Treatment commonly includes antipsychotic medication and psychotherapy.</p><p>Delusional disorder is a psychotic disorder that features the presence of one or more delusions for 1 month or longer with no other psychotic symptoms (eg, hallucinations, disorganized thinking, disorganized behaviors other than behaviors related to the delusion) or mood symptoms. Persecutory delusions (as in this patient) and somatic delusions are common types of delusions. Many patients will only present for care at the insistence of other people given their lack of insight into the psychotic nature of their concern. Treatment typically includes antipsychotic medication and psychotherapy with the goal of improving the patient's coping with the delusion and gradually increasing insight into the delusion. The prognosis is guarded.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Bipolar disorder (Choice A) is characterized by discrete episodes of depression and mania, which can sometimes be accompanied by psychotic symptoms, such as hallucinations and delusions. This patient does not report mood symptoms (eg, depressed or euphoric mood, increased or decreased sleep), Brief psychotic disorder (Choice B) is characterized by at least one acute psychotic symptom (eg, delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior) lasting less than 1 month. This patient's delusion has persisted for 6 months. Schizoaffective disorder (Choice D) and schizophrenia (Choice E) are chronic psychotic disorders characterized by delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, and negative symptoms (eg, alogia, apathy, flat affect). Schizoaffective disorder also includes significant mood episodes (major depressive, manic episodes). This patient demonstrates an isolated delusion with no disorganization of speech/thinking, hallucinations, or negative symptoms.</p>", "20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Fragile X syndrome is an X-linked dominant cause of inherited intellectual disability that presents due to a CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene. It presents with clinical features including intellectual disability, an elongated face with a prominent jaw, a high-arched palate, large ears, hyperextensible joints, and macroorchidism.</p><p>This patient's constellation of findings, including intellectual disability, an elongated face, large ears, and macroorchidism, is suggestive of fragile X syndrome. lu! Patients may also present with hyperextensible joints and a high-arched palate. Fragile X syndrome is the second most common cause of inherited intellectual disability after Down syndrome and is inherited in an X-linked dominant pattern. It is caused by a CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion within the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome. On karyotype, the end of the X chromosome appears truncated, leading to the name fragile X syndrome. Patients with fragile X syndrome are at increased risk for mitral valve prolapse and educational difficulties. They are often diagnosed with autism. As with all trinucleotide repeat expansions, genetic anticipation is seen, wherein future generations have increased severity and/or earlier onset of disease.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Congenital rubella (Choice A) presents in neonates with the classic triad of cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and patent ductus arteriosus. It is unlikely to be a cause of intellectual disability presenting in childhood. MECP2 gene mutation (Choice C) is the cause of Rett syndrome, characterized by regression of developmental milestones, and occurs exclusively in females. After an initially normal development, patients subsequently lose speech capabilities and motor skills, and develop stereotypical hand movements, gait abnormalities, and developmental delay. Autistic features may be present but abnormal facial features are not seen. Microdeletion on chromosome 7 (Choice D), specifically 7q11.23, is the cause of Williams syndrome. It presents with an overly friendly demeanor, emotional dysregulation, intellectual delay, elfin facies, short stature, vascular and cardiac valve abnormalities secondary to elastin dysfunction, structural abnormalities of the urinary tract, and endocrine abnormalities (eg, hypothyroidism). Prenatal alcohol exposure (Choice E) leads to irreversible fetal neurologic effects and physical malformations in a cluster of symptoms called fetal alcohol syndrome. Due to the failure of cell migration, fetal alcohol syndrome presents with physical abnormalities such as microcephaly, facial abnormalities (small palpebral fissures, thin vermilion border, cleft lip/palate, smooth or long philtrum), limb deformities, and congenital heart disease. This patient's physical features are more characteristic of fragile X syndrome.</p>", "21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Conversion disorder, also known as functional neurological symptom disorder, involves neurologic symptoms such as sensory or motor dysfunction that are not fully explained by objective findings on physical examination or imaging. Conversion disorder is postulated to represent the subconscious conversion of intolerable emotional distress into neurologic symptoms.</p><p>Conversion disorder, also known as functional neurological symptom disorder, involves neurologic symptoms such as sensory or motor dysfunction that are not fully explained by objective findings on physical examination or imaging. This patient demonstrates midline splitting of sensing vibration on her forehead, a symptom that cannot be localized and therefore represents a classic symptom of conversion disorder, along with facial paralysis that does not spare the forehead, which is associated with Bell palsy rather than a central nervous system lesion. Finally, her left-sided hemiplegia and hemisensory deficit, including left-sided facial motor and sensory deficits, cannot be localized (eg, brainstem lesions typically lead to ipsilateral cranial nerve dysfunction with contralateral dysfunction of the body). Conversion disorder is postulated to represent the subconscious conversion of intolerable emotional distress (eg, this patient's distress about being involved in a motor vehicle collision) into neurologic symptoms. The pathogenesis likely involves the top-down amplification of symptoms secondary to abnormal processing of negative emotions. Physicians should conduct a thorough history and physical examination as well as obtain laboratory and imaging studies to evaluate for medical Ae anbenlanig Ainnnnn Gialinniy AuAlnis celnmctiniks AUAINIA Win pian ninm wan Hin didannntn by tmalane Ih tenienntin Aniaonnminnt Aieeiimntinana Wa dinananintn ann ‘onversion disorder is postulated to represent the subconscious conversion of intolerable emotional distress (eg, this patient's distress about being involved ina - motor vehicle collision) into neurologic symptoms. The pathogenesis likely involves the top-down amplification of symptoms secondary to abnormal processing of negative emotions. Physicians should conduct a thorough history and physical examination as well as obtain laboratory and imaging studies to evaluate for medical or neurologic disease that may explain or partially explain the symptoms when the diagnosis is unclear. In traumatic or emergent circumstances, the diagnosis is one of exclusion. Treatment of conversion disorder involves education about the diagnosis, physical therapy, and cognitive behavioral therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Carotid artery dissection (Choice A), which can occur secondary to whiplash injury, commonly leads to ischemic stroke and carotid sinus syncope secondary to pressure against the carotid sinus baroreceptors. However, this patient does not demonstrate a localizable pattern of neurologic deficits or syncope. In somatization disorder (Choice C), also known as somatic symptom disorder, the patient is excessively and persistently preoccupied with one or more somatic symptoms such that these symptoms disrupt the patient's daily life. This patient has not been persistently preoccupied with her new-onset blindness. In addition, if somatic symptoms are neurologic in nature, conversion disorder is a more precise diagnosis than somatic symptom disorder. Subdural hematoma (Choice D) is defined by bleeding between the dura and arachnoid mater. In severe cases, the subdural hematoma may be large enough to compress the brain parenchyma, which can result in progressive neurologic deficits with upper motor neuron signs such as a Babinski sign, altered mental status, seizures, herniation syndromes, and signs of increased intracranial pressure (eg, papilledema). This patient does not demonstrate a Babinski sign, evidence of increased intracranial pressure, altered mental status, or other signs of a large subdural hematoma. Vertebral artery dissection (Choice E), which rarely occurs secondary to whiplash injury, commonly leads to thromboembolic strokes of the posterior circulation. Patients typically present with vestibulocerebellar signs (ataxia, dysmetria, dizziness, imbalance, vertigo, vomiting, nystagmus), ipsilateral cranial nerve dysfunction, contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral decrease in pain and temperature sensation, contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing, and/or Horner syndrome. This patient lacks vestibulocerebellar signs, and her neurologic symptoms are ipsilateral.</p>", "22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute hypomania and mania typically manifest with an irritable or euphoric mood, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility, and grandiose thinking. Patients with underlying bipolar disorder commonly present initially with depressive episodes.</p><p>This patient with a history of recurrent depressive episodes is presenting with new-onset hypomanic symptoms, consistent with a diagnosis of bipolar disorder. Bipolar disorder, type I, is a mood disorder featuring acute episodes of mania and may include episodes of depression. Bipolar disorder, type Il, is a mood disorder featuring episodes of major depressive disorder and hypomania. Patients with bipolar disorder commonly present initially with depressive episodes and may be provisionally diagnosed with major depressive disorder before experiencing hypomanic/manic episodes, revealing their underlying bipolar disorder. Acute mania manifests with four to five of the following symptoms: irritable or euphoric mood, increased goal-directed activity, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, rapid sneech and thinkina. irresnonsibilitv. and arandiose thinkina. Hvnomanic enisodes are like manic enisodes but less severe and do not markedly imnair social 36 featuring episodes of major depressive disorder and hypomania. Patients with bipolar disorder commonly present initially with depressive episodes and may be provisionally diagnosed with major depressive disorder before experiencing hypomanic/manic episodes, revealing their underlying bipolar disorder. Acute mania manifests with four to five of the following symptoms: irritable or euphoric mood, increased goal-directed activity, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility, and grandiose thinking. Hypomanic episodes are like manic episodes but less severe and do not markedly impair social functioning or require hospitalization. This patient demonstrates pressured speech, impulsive behavior outside of her normal behavior (multiple piercings) that could be considered irresponsible, grandiose thinking as evidenced in her statement about her “perfect” blood pressure and weight, rapid thinking (as evidenced in her frequent topic changes when speaking), and increased goal-directed activity (wanting to train in design and lose weight). Management of acute hypomania typically requires mood stabilizing therapy (eg, mood stabilizers or mood stabilizing atypical antipsychotics).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Bulimia nervosa (Choice B) involves cycles of uncontrollable eating and compensatory behaviors such as restricting or vomiting, laxative misuse, or diuretic overuse. This patient does not demonstrate recurrent disordered eating behaviors. Her focus on losing weight is acute and is happening in the context of manic symptoms. Cyclothymic disorder (Choice C) presents with hypomanic and depressive symptoms that fail to meet diagnostic criteria for a hypomanic or depressive episode. This patient's symptoms meet criteria for a hypomanic episode, and she has a history of major depressive episodes. Delusional disorder, erotomanic type (Choice D) presents with fixed delusions of a sexual nature in the absence of other psychiatric symptoms such as hallucinations, disorganization, depression, or mania. This patient demonstrates clear manic symptoms. Histrionic personality disorder (Choice E), a cluster B personality disorder, is characterized by theatrical, superficial expressions of emotion that unconsciously serve to garner attention from others to fulfill emotional needs. Narcissistic personality disorder (Choice F) is a cluster B personality disorder that is characterized by fragile self-esteem and compensatory arrogant, self-aggrandizing behavior to gain admiration, sometimes at others’ expense. Personality disorder symptoms are chronic and pervasive, whereas this patient's dramatic grandiosity is acute and occurring in the setting of manic symptoms.</p>", "23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Benzodiazepine withdrawal is associated with sympathetic overdrive and presents with tremors, anxiety, diaphoresis, hypertension, and tachycardia.</p><p>Benzodiazepines such as lorazepam increase the central nervous system activity of y-aminobutyric acid, the main inhibitory neurotransmitter. Abrupt discontinuation of lorazepam therefore leads to relative excess of excitatory pathways. Benzodiazepine withdrawal typically presents with tremors, anxiety, restlessness, headache, nausea, and diaphoresis, which can occur hours after the cessation of use. Withdrawal is managed supportively with benzodiazepines to address agitation and prevent the symptoms of withdrawal, intravenous fluids, nutritional supplementation (especially if alcohol is jointly used), and frequent assessment including vital sign checks. Other complications of benzodiazepine withdrawal include hallucinosis (auditory or visual hallucinations without confusion or autonomic instability) and seizures.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Dysregulation of acetylcholine (Choice A), such as with organophosphates or other inhibitors of acetylcholinesterase, leads to excess concentrations of acetylcholine in the neuromuscular junction. This can result in vomiting, diarrhea, urination, diaphoresis, salivation, lacrimation, bronchorrhea, bronchospasm, bradycardia, seizures, and muscle weakness. Dopamine (Choice C) dysregulation, such as with cocaine use, causes increased synaptic dopamine, which can lead to hallucinations and paranoia. Chronic opiate use can lead to inhibition of endogenous opioid (Choice D). Opiate withdrawal presents with restlessness, tachycardia and hypertension, pupil dilation, tremors, muscle cramping, abdominal cramping, nausea and vomiting, and diarrhea. Lacrimation and rhinorrhea can occur. Opiate withdrawal also features yawning and piloerection. Serotonin syndrome is the result of serotonin toxicity (Choice E), which can lead to the triad of acute confusion, autonomic hyperactivity (hyperthermia, tachycardia, hypertension), and neuromuscular excitability (tremor, myoclonus, hyperreflexia). Due to serotonin receptors in the gastrointestinal tract, vomiting and diarrhea can also occur.</p>", "24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Diphenhydramine is an antihistamine with anticholinergic activity sometimes used for insomnia. Constipation is a relatively common adverse effect. In the case of severe or bothersome constipation, diphenhydramine should be discontinued.</p><p>This patient is likely experiencing constipation as an adverse effect of diphenhydramine. Thus, discontinuing diphenhydramine is the next management step. Diphenhydramine is an antihistamine with anticholinergic activity used in the treatment of allergic reactions and utilized off-label for anxiety and insomnia. Endogenous acetylcholine binding to muscarinic receptors causes smooth muscle contraction of the sweat glands, salivary glands, airways, bladder, and bowel. Cholinergic neurons projecting to the cerebral cortex mediate attention, learning, and memory. Antagonism of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors blocks cholinergic signaling in the brain and causes the dysfunction of smooth muscle contraction, leading to cognitive impairment, delirium, sedation, anhidrosis, dry mouth, bronchospasm, urinary retention, and/or constipation. Common constipation symptoms include having fewer than three bowel movements weekly, passing hard stools, straining to defecate, and experiencing abdominal bloating. When medications cause bothersome or severe constipation, the offending medication should be decreased or discontinued.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Anorectal manometry (Choice A) evaluates the function of the anal sphincter. Colon transit studies (Choice B) assist in assessing colonic motility. Both can diagnose the cause of constipation. However, this patient has a likely cause of constipation, so these tests are unnecessary pending trial of discontinuation of diphenhydramine. Initiation of fiber supplementation (Choice D) can help with idiopathic, chronic constipation. However, this patient has a reversible cause. X-ray of the abdomen (Choice E) can assess stool burden. It can be helpful in confirming a diagnosis of constipation when the diagnosis is unclear. However, in this patient, constipation can be diagnosed clinically given her classic symptoms of constipation.</p>", "25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Though antipsychotic medications are the mainstay of schizophrenia treatment, adjunctive psychosocial treatments help as well. As family conflict is a risk factor for psychotic symptom exacerbations, family therapy can be beneficial.</p><p>According to the DSM-5, patients with schizophrenia demonstrate at least two of the following five symptoms: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, or negative symptoms (eg, flat affect, apathy, alogia). To meet diagnostic criteria, patients must demonstrate functional deficits, and the total duration of symptoms must exceed 6 months. Family conflict and other psychological stressors are risk factors for worsening psychosis. Stress can increase patients’ negative interpretations of hallucinations and decrease patients’ emotional resources for effectively coping with psychotic symptoms, impacting their daily functioning. Treatment of schizophrenia requires antipsychotic medications, though adjunctive psychosocial supports including family therapy, cognitive behavioral therapy, and social skills training can be beneficial. Family therapy focuses on psychoeducation, encouraging mutual understanding and effective communication, and teaching skills for the patient and family to cope with psychotic symptoms.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Recommending partial hospitalization program (Choice A) is not necessary currently. First, the physician should address factors impacting the patient's symptoms and functioning on an outpatient basis, such as family conflict. Recommending that he move to a halfway house (Choice B) is not appropriate. This patient has no criminal history. Adding lithium carbonate to his current regimen (Choice D) is unlikely to address this patient's psychotic symptoms. Lithium carbonate is sometimes used in schizoaffective disorder to address mood symptoms, but lithium carbonate is unhelpful in schizophrenia. Adding lorazepam to his current regimen (Choice E) may help assuage anxiety symptoms but does not address psychotic symptoms or the likely root of this patient's psychosis exacerbation, the family conflict. Family therapy is more likely to be helpful. Discontinuing haloperidol and beginning clozapine (Choice F) is not necessary currently. This patient's family conflict, the immediate stressor that correlates with the psychosis exacerbation, should be addressed first. Following this, the physician should consider increasing the dose or frequency of haloperidol (Choice G).</p>", "26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Though patients may make decisions that physicians deem as poor medical judgment, the ethical principle of autonomy dictates that patients have the right to make these decisions. Coercive treatment is only warranted when patients experience medical or psychiatric emergencies that interfere with their ability to make decisions.</p><p>Physicians should respect patient autonomy. This principle is especially pertinent when the patient is an adult without evidence of psychiatric or medical conditions that impair basic decision-making capabilities. Though patients may make decisions physicians deem as poor medical judgment, the ethical principle of autonomy dictates that patients have the right to make these decisions. Coercive treatment is only warranted when patients experience medical or psychiatric conditions that interfere with their ability to make decisions. This patient has intact cognitive function and appears to possess decisional capacity to end hemodialysis in that he is able to appropriately verbalize his reasoning for ending hemodialysis (valuing his quality of life).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Contacting the patient’s daughter, son, or wife (Choices A, B, and C) is unnecessary, as the physician should respect the patient's autonomy. If the patient lacked decisional capacity, his durable power of attorney (his son) (Choice B) should be contacted first for substitute decision making. However, the patient is a consenting adult who appears to have decisional capacity to end hemodialysis. Consulting the state probate court (Choice D) would only be warranted if the patient lacked decisional capacity and had no next of kin (substitute decision makers).</p>", "27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with illness anxiety disorder demonstrate persistent and excessive anxiety about the possibility of having or acquiring a serious illness such that they perform frequent health-related behaviors. Compared with patients with illness anxiety disorder, patients with somatic symptom disorder are typically more preoccupied with existing somatic symptoms than the possibility they may have a serious illness.</p><p>Adisproportionate fear of serious disease is consistent with illness anxiety disorder. According to the DSM-5, patients with illness anxiety disorder demonstrate excessive anxiety and preoccupation about the possibility that they may have or acquire a serious illness such that they perform frequent health-related behaviors (eg, repeatedly going to the doctor) or exhibit maladaptive avoidance (eg, completely avoiding going to the doctor). This preoccupation persists for at least 6 months. Management centers around regular primary care follow-up evaluation that targets coping skills, provides reassurance, and avoids unnecessary tests.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Body dysmorphic disorder (Choice A) manifests as preoccupation with a perceived flaw in physical appearance leading to repetitive behaviors (eg, checking) or mental acts (eg, comparing appearance to others). This patient is less concerned about her appearance than the possibility that she has a serious illness. Generalized anxiety disorder (Choice B) is an anxiety disorder that features excessive worrying about a range of topics with psychological and physical symptoms and associated functional impairment. This patient's worry is specific to the possibility of having a serious illness. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (Choice D) is an anxiety disorder typically characterized by obsessions (unwanted, intrusive thoughts that produce anxiety) that the patient attempts to neutralize with compulsions (repetitive behaviors). Compulsions are typically ego-dystonic (recognizable by the patient as abnormal or excessive), whereas this patient's mole-checking behavior is ego-syntonic (she does not view the checking as problematic). As well, this patient's anxiety-driven behaviors logically stem from her concern about having melanoma, whereas there is commonly less of a logical link between obsessions and compulsions in obsessive-compulsive disorder. In somatic symptom disorder (Choice E), the patient is preoccupied with existing somatic symptoms such that these symptoms disrupt the patient's daily life. Patients with somatic symptom disorder persistently devote excessive time and energy to these symptoms or related health concerns (eg, repeatedly going to the doctor). Patients with illness anxiety disorder typically have milder somatic symptoms relative to their concern about developing a serious illness.</p>", "28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is a sequela of chronic lithium use. Lithium impairs the ability of the collecting tubule to reclaim free water by interfering with the pathway of antidiuretic hormone in the cells. Patients typically present with polydipsia, polyuria, hyperosmolar serum, hypernatremia, dilute urine, and failure to respond to desmopressin.</p><p>Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (NDI) is commonly acquired secondary to electrolyte disturbance or medications (eg, chronic lithium use) that render the renal collecting duct insensitive to antidiuretic hormone (ADH). Normally, ADH triggers the insertion of aquaporin channels into the collecting duct membrane via a cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)-mediated pathway. Over time, lithium concentrates within the cells of the collecting duct and interferes with this signaling pathway, leading to limited aquaporin insertion and compromised ability of the kidney to reclaim free water. Dehydration results, as found in this patient, along with symptoms of polydipsia (compensatory response to low serum volume or increased serum osmolarity) and polyuria or incontinence (due to the large volume of dilute urine produced). In severe cases of dehydration, vital signs may show tachycardia and hypotension. Physical examination is generally unremarkable, apart from potential signs of volume depletion (eg, weak pulse, skin tenting, altered mental status). Laboratory studies show increased serum osmolarity, hypernatremia, and inappropriately dilute urine. Normally, urine should be concentrated in states of hyperosmolar serum, as the main physiologic function of ADH is to reclaim water, thereby maintaining serum osmolarity and sodium balance in addition to plasma volume. Desmopressin, an ADH analog, is used as a means of differentiating nephrogenic from central diabetes insipidus. Central diabetes insipidus occurs due to an absence of ADH production, not a failure of the renal collecting tubule to respond to it. If urine output and serum osmolarity decrease following desmopressin administration, a central cause of diabetes insipidus (eg, head trauma, hypothalamic damage, pituitary tumor) should be investigated. No change in response to desmopressin indicates that the cause of diabetes insipidus is nephrogenic. The treatment of NDI may include volume repletion plus thiazide diuretics.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Acute lithium toxicity (Choice A) is characterized by neurologic (eg, somnolence, confusion, ataxia, nystagmus, coarse tremors, seizures), gastrointestinal, and cardiac manifestations (eg, bradycardia). Because lithium is similar in charge and size to sodium and exclusively metabolized by the kidney, conditions that increase renal reabsorption of sodium or decrease renal function can increase serum lithium concentrations and the risk for lithium toxicity. Causes include the use of thiazide diuretics, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, ACE inhibitors, and hypovolemia. Lithium-induced hypothyroidism (Choice C) occurs in as many as 50% of patients being treated with lithium, usually within 1 to 2 years of beginning treatment. Hyperthyroidism and autoimmune thyroiditis may also occur. While this patient's TSH is mildly increased, hypernatremia, polyuria, and inappropriately dilute urine are characteristic of NDI, not hypothyroidism. Routine side effects due to lithium treatment (Choice D) include tremor, nystagmus, polyuria, and mild thyroid dysfunction. While this patient does have polyuria, her dehydration, altered mental status, hypernatremia, and inappropriately dilute urine are concerning for a potentially life-threatening condition and should not be dismissed as routine. Type 2 diabetes mellitus (Choice E) often presents with polyuria due to increased amounts of glucose in the proximal convoluted tubule, which causes osmotic diuresis. While this patient does have polyuria, hypernatremia and a history of chronic lithium use are more consistent with NDI.</p>", "29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Alcohol use disorder is characterized by an inability to cut back on drinking, excessive time spent drinking, and negative consequences related to drinking. Patients with moderate to severe alcohol use disorder may benefit from daily medication. Naltrexone is particularly helpful for patients who want to cut back on but not completely discontinue alcohol use.</p><p>Alcohol use disorder refers to an addiction to alcohol that must meet at least two criteria, including an inability to cut back on drinking, drinking more than expected, excessive time spent drinking, alcohol cravings, persistent drinking despite physical or mental problems related to drinking, or persistent drinking despite negative social consequences. Though patients with mild alcohol use disorder may rely solely on psychosocial supports such as 12-step programs, patients with moderate to severe alcohol use disorder or patients unable to access or tolerate psychosocial supports may benefit from daily medication. Commonly used medications for alcohol use disorder include naltrexone, acamprosate, disulfiram, and gabapentin. Naltrexone, a long-acting opioid receptor antagonist, blocks the rewarding effects of drinking alcohol and thinking about alcohol, thereby preventing alcohol cravings. Naltrexone can be used in conjunction with alcohol and is therefore helpful for patients (like this patient) interested in moderating their drinking but unsure whether they want to quit.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Amitriptyline (Choice A) and sertraline (Choice E) are antidepressants used in major depressive disorder and anxiety disorders. They are not helpful in alcohol use disorder. Clonazepam (Choice B) is a benzodiazepine medication used to treat anxiety. Though other benzodiazepines (eg, lorazepam, chlordiazepoxide) are commonly used in alcohol withdrawal, this patient has not yet committed to quitting alcohol, so a medication to help him is more appropriate. Disulfiram (Choice C) inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, causing acetaldehyde accumulation, which leads to unpleasant physical effects, including headache, fatigue, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, and hypotension. It is helpful in patients who are motivated to stay sober from alcohol but not patients who are unsure about completely discontinuing their alcohol use.</p>", "30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Brief psychotic disorder is characterized by at least one acute psychotic symptom (eg, delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior) lasting less than 1 month.</p><p>This patient meets the diagnostic criteria for brief psychotic disorder. Brief psychotic disorder is characterized by the acute onset of one or more psychotic symptoms (eg, delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior) lasting less than 1 month. This patient was experiencing paranoid delusions. Risk factors for brief psychotic disorder include stressful life events and personality disorders. Treatment may include hospitalization and antipsychotic treatment depending on symptom severity and functional impairment, though some symptoms or the entire presentation may resolve without medications.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Adjustment disorder with mixed disturbance of emotions and conduct (Choice A) refers to the onset of disproportionate distress within 3 months of an identifiable stressor with associated anxiety, depression, and conduct disturbances. Psychotic symptoms would be atypical. Delusional disorder (Choice C) is a psychotic disorder that features the presence of one or more delusions for 1 month or longer with no other psychotic symptoms. This patient's psychotic symptoms lasted 1 week only. I Post-traumatic stress disorder (Choice D) refers to symptoms of intrusive thoughts/memories of trauma, hyperarousal, avoidance of trauma reminders, and/or negative mood/thoughts resulting from a traumatic event such as a life-threatening motor vehicle collision and lasting more than 1 month. This patient did not experience a life-threatening incident, intrusive memories, avoidance of trauma reminders, or negative changes in mood. Patients with schizophreniform disorder (Choice E) must demonstrate two of the following: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, or disorganized behavior (known as positive symptoms), or negative symptoms (eg, flat affect, apathy, alogia). To meet diagnostic criteria, patients must demonstrate symptoms for more than 1 month and less than 6 months. This patient does not meet the duration criteria for schizophreniform disorder. Substance-induced psychotic disorder (Choice F) refers to psychotic symptoms secondary to substance use. Alcohol use rarely leads directly to psychotic symptoms. Though heavy cannabis use can lead to psychotic symptoms, this patient's cannabis use is intermittent.</p>", "31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients who are making high-stakes or risky medical decisions (eg, declining a life-saving procedure) should be evaluated for decision- making capacity. To meet the criteria for decisional capacity, patients must express a consistent choice, demonstrate an understanding of the risks and benefits of the choice, demonstrate an appreciation of the significance of the choice, and illustrate an ability to reason through the options.</p><p>Patients who are making high-stakes or risky medical decisions (eg, declining a life-saving procedure) should be informally or formally evaluated for decision-making capacity. This patient demonstrates abnormalities on cognitive testing and has risk factors for cognitive impairment, including traumatic brain injury and prior illicit drug use. Cognitive impairment confers a higher risk for a lack of decision-making capacity. To meet the criteria for decision-making capacity, patients must express a consistent choice, demonstrate an understanding of the risks and benefits of the choice, demonstrate an appreciation of the significance of the choice for their personal circumstances, and illustrate an ability to reason through the options. The medical team should assess the patient's decision-making capacity for each decision at each point in time. If decision-making capacity is present, the medical team should respect the patient's choice. If decision-making capacity is absent, the medical team should first try to restore decision-making capacity (eg, treat psychosis). If decision-making capacity cannot be restored, then the medical team needs to rely on substituted judgment from durable powers of attorney or next of kin.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Contact the hospital ethics committee for a clinical decision (Choice A) is unnecessary at this point. The medical team should first assess the patient's decision- Co making capacity. If decision-making capacity is absent and the patient has no durable power of attorney or next of kin, then the hospital ethics committee should be contacted. Defer the decision to prison authorities (Choice B) is unethical. Prison authorities, involved in the patient's punishment, should play no role in medical decision making. Obtain complete neurocognitive testing (Choice D) may be helpful in elucidating the reason for the patient's abnormalities on cognitive screening. However, the most urgent issue is determining the patient's decision-making capacity to decline the aortic valve replacement procedure. Transition the patient to comfort care only (Choice E) is premature. The medical team should first assess the patient's decision-making capacity to decline the aortic valve replacement procedure.</p>", "32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with major depressive disorder, including major depressive disorder with anxious features, should be treated with antidepressant medication rather than benzodiazepines. In some patients, lorazepam can lead to rebound anxiety. In older patients, lorazepam carries a risk for delirium, and thus it should be avoided.</p><p>Lorazepam, a benzodiazepine with a short half-life, can lead to rebound anxiety, resulting in this patient's increased lorazepam use. Also, lorazepam commonly leads to delirium in older patients. Thus, lorazepam should be discontinued. This patient who reports a sad mood, insomnia, decreased appetite, decreased energy, and decreased motivation/activity likely meets criteria for major depressive disorder (MDD). MDD is characterized by 2 or more weeks of at least five of the following symptoms: depressed mood, anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor slowing, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). These symptoms disrupt everyday functioning. Patients commonly have negative thoughts about ~ lead O delirium older patients. , lOfazZepam should De discontinued. This patie No repo a Sad mood, insomnia, decreased appetite, decreased energy, « and decreased motivation/activity likely meets criteria for major depressive disorder (MDD). MDD is characterized by 2 or more weeks of at least five of the following symptoms: depressed mood, anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor slowing, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). These symptoms disrupt everyday functioning. Patients commonly have negative thoughts about themselves, others, and the world. Patients seeing themselves as a burden also constitutes an acute risk factor for suicide. Older men are at particularly high risk for completing suicide, so this patient should be carefully evaluated for suicidal ideation. Many depressed patients engage in anxious rumination. MDD that includes anxiety symptoms can be termed “MDD with anxious features” per the DSM-5. Initial antidepressant medication for most patients is selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor therapy. However, for patients with the combination of decreased appetite and insomnia, mirtazapine is another reasonable first-line option given its adverse effect profile that includes weight gain and sedation. Mirtazapine, an atypical antidepressant medication, is a selective a,-receptor antagonist and serotonin modulator.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Continue current regimen for 3 weeks and reassure the patient (Choice A) would be inappropriate given that lorazepam has been ineffective for the patient's anxiety and carries a delirium risk for older patients. As well, the patient demonstrates depressive symptoms that are unlikely to respond to lorazepam only. Continue lorazepam and add amitriptyline (Choice B) would be inappropriate given that lorazepam has been ineffective for the patient's anxiety and carries a delirium risk for older patients. Amitriptyline is a tricyclic antidepressant. Tricyclic antidepressants are not first-line options for MDD given their anticholinergic adverse effects and cardiac and seizure risks. The anticholinergic adverse effects are associated with delirium in older patients. Discontinue lorazepam and reevaluate after 1 week (Choice D) is a less optimal option than discontinuing lorazepam and starting mirtazapine. This patient has depressive symptoms that should be addressed with antidepressant medication. Increase the dosage of lorazepam to 2 mg as necessary (Choice E) is unlikely to be helpful and is likely to worsen the patient's rebound anxiety and delirium risk.</p>", "33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Exogenous hyperinsulinemia manifests with increased serum insulin concentration, hypoglycemia, and decreased serum C-peptide concentration. Exogenous insulin administration is consistent with a diagnosis of factitious disorder. In factitious disorder, patients consciously produce symptoms for primary gain.</p><p>A decreased serum C-peptide concentration indicates that exogenous insulin is being administered, consistent with a diagnosis of factitious disorder. The pancreas coproduces insulin and C-peptide. Measurement of serum C-peptide concentration can be used to diagnose diabetes mellitus and distinguish type 1 from type 2. It can also be used in diagnosing factitious hypoglycemia when a patient may be suspected of self-administering insulin. Though serum insulin concentration will appear increased (and blood glucose will consequently be decreased), negative feedback on the pancreas decreases production of C-peptide (and native insulin). In factitious disorder, patients consciously produce symptoms (eg, purposely injure themselves, fake a fever by eating something warm) for primary gain. Primary gain is the motivation to be cared for, which constitutes an unconscious motivator for the patient's conscious production of symptoms. Early losses, neglect, or abuse may disrupt patients’ identity and relationships, and adopting the sick role may represent patients’ attempts to cope with stress and stabilize their identity and relationships. Psychiatric comorbidities (eg, depression, substance use disorders, personality disorders) are common but not present in all patients. Diagnosis is supported by inconsistencies in the history or physical examination and a history of extensive clinical evaluations. Treatment includes regular follow-up visits with one physician who oversees management (as opposed to symptom-triggered appointments) and possible psychotherapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, E, F, G, and H. Autoimmune hypothyroidism (Choice A) is associated with type 1 diabetes mellitus, and thyroid dysfunction has been associated with insulin resistance. Insulin resistance syndrome (Choice E) describes sequelae of insulin resistance including type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension, dyslipidemia, and cardiovascular disease. Type 1 diabetes mellitus (Choice G) involves the autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, resulting in decreased insulin and C-peptide concentrations. Type 2 diabetes mellitus (Choice H) involves insulin resistance, which drives increased serum insulin and C-peptide concentrations. All these conditions are more likely to present with hyperglycemia than hypoglycemia. In diabetes insipidus (Choice B), a lack of antidiuretic hormone (central) or resistance to antidiuretic hormone (nephrogenic) leads to decreased free water reabsorption from collecting tubules, resulting in polyuria along with a state of dehydration and hypernatremia. Diabetes insipidus does not directly influence blood glucose or serum C-peptide concentrations. Primary hyperparathyroidism (Choice D) results in hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, subperiosteal bone resorption, and renal failure. Over time, primary hyperparathyroidism can lead to insulin resistance. However, insulin resistance most commonly presents with hyperglycemia and increased serum C-peptide and insulin concentrations. Insulinoma (Choice F) refers to a rare neoplasm of pancreatic islet cells that functionally produces insulin. These neoplastic cells classically do not respond to typical feedback mechanisms, and, as a result, insulin is continuously produced even in periods of hypoglycemia. Patients present with symptoms of hypoglycemia; however, serum C-peptide concentration would be increased.</p>", "34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vaginismus is characterized by pelvic floor muscle spasms and genito-pelvic pain leading to difficulty with vaginal penetration. Treatment primarily involves pelvic floor physical therapy, psychotherapy (eg, cognitive behavioral therapy), and psychoeducation.</p><p>This patient likely has vaginismus, involuntary muscle spasm during vaginal penetration. Patients are defined in the DSM-5 as having difficulty with vaginal intercourse, genito-pelvic pain, fear of vaginal penetration or pain, and increased pelvic floor muscle tension during attempted penetration. Risk factors include a history of abuse, anxiety disorders, relationship conflicts, and cultural taboos (though not all patients have these risk factors). Vaginismus can also happen secondary to vulval infections (eg, candidiasis), gynecological problems (eg, endometriosis), or pregnancy. Physical examination is typically normal other than pelvic floor muscle tightness. Treatment is multimodal and commonly involves pelvic floor physical therapy, psychotherapy (eg, cognitive behavioral therapy), psychoeducation, and/or topical pain medication.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Generalized anxiety disorder (Choice A) is characterized by 6 months or more of excessive worrying about a range of topics and is associated with three additional psychological symptoms (irritability, restlessness, difficulty concentrating, fatigability, sleep disturbance) and/or muscle tension. This patient's anxiety is specific to vaginal penetration. In somatic symptom disorder (Choice B), the patient is excessively and persistently preoccupied with one or more somatic symptoms such that these symptoms disrupt the patient's daily life. Patients typically devote excessive time and energy to these symptoms or related health concerns (eg, repeatedly going to the doctor). This patient does not demonstrate excessive health-seeking behaviors or preoccupation other than when she is attempting vaginal penetration. Vaginal candidiasis (Choice C) is associated with vaginismus. However, pelvic examination typically shows vulvar and vaginal erythema and edema and thick white discharge. As well, this patient lacks risk factors for vaginal candidiasis (eg, antibiotic use, diabetes mellitus, immunosuppressed status). Vulvodynia (Choice E) refers to vulvar pain (or burning or stinging) of unknown etiology, not vaginal pain. This pain is not typically provoked by vaginal penetration.</p>", "35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Phencyclidine, also known as PCP, is an NMDA glutamatergic receptor antagonist that is used recreationally for its euphoria-inducing and dissociative effects. PCP intoxication is often characterized by signs and symptoms including hyperthermia, hypertension, tachycardia, erratic behavior, paranoia, psychosis, aggression, decreased sensation, ataxia, nystagmus, myoclonus, and dysarthria.</p><p>Phencyclidine, also known as PCP, is an NMDA glutamatergic receptor antagonist that is used recreationally for its euphoria-inducing and dissociative effects. PCP intoxication is often characterized by signs and symptoms including hyperthermia, hypertension, tachycardia, erratic behavior, paranoia, psychosis, aggression, decreased sensation, ataxia, nystagmus, myoclonus, and dysarthria. Patients may require sedation, treatment with antipsychotic medications, or physical restraint until their symptoms subside. Withdrawal symptoms following PCP intoxication are uncommon. Naloxone has no effect on the pathways involved in PCP. intoxication.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Alcohol intoxication (Choice A) presents with sedation, altered mentation, dysarthria, and vomiting. Alcohol withdrawal may lead to severe complications including delirium tremens and seizures. Alcohol intoxication does not typically present with nystagmus; hyperthermia and autonomic instability would also be uncommon in acute intoxication, though the vasodepressive and sedating effects can result in hypotension and bradypnea. Amphetamine intoxication (Choice B) may resemble PCP intoxication and also presents with hyperthermia, hypertension, tachycardia, erratic behavior, and paranoid or delusional thinking. However, it does not typically present with nystagmus, ataxia, or myoclonus. Opioid intoxication (Choice C) may result in erratic behavior, miosis, respiratory depression, and obtundation but should respond rapidly to administration of naloxone. Failure of respiratory depression and mental status to improve following naloxone administration should lead to consideration of alternative diagnoses. Schizophrenia (Choice E) presents with hallucinations and delusional, disorganized thinking and behavior. Schizophrenia symptoms are typically chronic. Nystagmus, ataxia, and myoclonus are not typical of schizophrenia.</p>", "36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder that features acute episodes of mania and may include episodes of depression. Acute mania typically manifests with an irritable or euphoric mood, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility, and grandiose thinking. Some patients demonstrate associated psychotic symptoms.</p><p>This patient is experiencing an episode of acute mania and can therefore be diagnosed with bipolar disorder. Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder that features acute episodes of mania and may include episodes of depression. Acute mania typically manifests with an irritable or euphoric mood, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility (as evidenced by this patient's sexual advances and overspending), and grandiose thinking. Some patients demonstrate associated psychotic symptoms (eg, grandiose delusions as in this patient, hallucinations). Acute mania can severely disrupt patients’ daily function and endanger their safety. Management of acute mania typically requires mood stabilizing therapy (eg, mood stabilizers or mood stabilizing atypical antipsychotics).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (Choice A) presents with chronic symptoms of hyperactivity/impulsivity and/or inattention that occur in more than one setting and impair academic, social, and/or emotional function. This patient instead presents with acutely hyperactive and impulsive behavior along with several other symptoms consistent with acute mania. iil Delusional disorder (Choice C) presents with fixed delusions in the absence of other psychiatric symptoms such as hallucinations, disorganization, depression, or mania. Histrionic personality disorder (Choice D), a cluster B personality disorder, is characterized by theatrical, superficial expressions of emotion that unconsciously serve to garner attention from others to fulfill emotional needs. Personality disorder symptoms are chronic and pervasive, whereas this patient's seductive behavior is occurring in the setting of acute manic symptoms. Schizophrenia (Choice E) presents with at least two of the following five symptoms: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, or negative symptoms (eg, flat affect, apathy, alogia). This patient does not demonstrate negative symptoms, instead presenting with acute manic symptoms consistent with bipolar disorder.</p>", "37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Delirium is an acute confusional state resulting from acute medical illness, sleep disturbances, substance use, and/or medications. Delirium presents with acute disturbances in awareness, attention, and baseline cognition that fluctuate in severity during the course of the day and may also present with perceptual disturbances such as visual hallucinations.</p><p>Delirium is an acute confusional state typically resulting from acute medical illness, sleep disturbances, substance use, and/or medications. Delirium is especially likely in older patients who possess a decreased cognitive reserve, though it is also likely in younger patients with acute medical illness (as in this patient with a high fever and right upper quadrant abdominal pain who may have a serious infection such as acute cholecystitis or cholangitis). Delirium presents with acute disturbances in awareness, attention, and baseline cognition that fluctuate in severity during the course of the day. Patients may present with perceptual disturbances (eg, hallucinations, delusions). Management of delirium should focus on treatment of the underlying medical condition if one is detected, frequent reorientation, maintenance of normal sleep-wake cycles, and placement of the patient in a calm and soothing environment.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Alcohol withdrawal (Choice A) can present with acute confusion in the case of delirium tremens. Delirium tremens typically presents with autonomic instability (eg, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypertension, hyperthermia), tremors, anxiety, hallucinations, confusion, delirium, and ataxia, and may result in convulsions, coma, and death. However, this patient's moderate use of alcohol is unlikely to lead to a severe withdrawal syndrome including delirium tremens. As well, the patient has the localizing symptom of abdominal pain; in addition to his fever, this symptom suggests an acute abdominal infection (alcoholic hepatitis is less likely given his moderate use of alcohol). Cerebral infarction (Choice B) is unlikely in this young, relatively healthy patient. As well, his fever and abdominal pain are inconsistent with cerebral infarction. Porphyria (Choice D) is a group of disorders of heme synthesis characterized by accumulation of heme precursors and may present with dark-colored urine, gastrointestinal distress, and blistering of the skin. This patient does not have blistering skin. Patients with schizophreniform disorder (Choice E) must demonstrate two of following: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, or disorganized behavior (known as positive symptoms), or negative symptoms (eg, flat affect, apathy, alogia). Patients with schizophreniform disorder do not typically present with acute fluctuations in awareness, attention, and cognitive symptoms such as orientation.</p>", "38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder presents with chronic symptoms of hyperactivity/impulsivity and/or inattention that occur in more than one setting and impair academic, social, and/or emotional function. Diagnosis requires that parents and teachers fill out behavioral rating scales.</p><p>Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a heritable neurodevelopmental disorder that presents with chronic symptoms of hyperactivity/impulsivity and/or inattention that occur in more than one setting and impair academic, social, and/or emotional function. Children with predominant hyperactivity symptoms are unable to sit still and may have difficulty with impulse control (postulated to result from a dysregulated dopamine reward pathway), resulting in movement initiation without planning and consequent injuries, as in this patient. Some patients with ADHD may demonstrate body-focused activities (eg, nose picking, skin picking) to relieve . their inner sense of restlessness. Children with predominant inattentive symptoms tend to daydream, process information slowly, and have difficulty completing tasks (related to norepinephrine dysregulation). This patient demonstrates predominant hyperactive symptoms. For diagnosis, physicians must establish that the patient is functionally impaired in several settings, so parents and teachers must fill out behavioral rating scales. Stimulants, which include amphetamine salts and methylphenidate, are first-line agents for treatment that increase synaptic dopamine and norepinephrine. Sleep problems, including comorbid primary sleep disorders (eg, insomnia) and stimulant-induced sleep problems, are common in patients with ADHD.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Allergy skin tests (Choice B) help assess whether a particular substance is causing a patient's atopic and/or allergic symptomatology. Clotting factor tests (Choice C) help assess for bleeding disorders. This patient's ecchymosis is more likely due to impulse dysregulation in the setting of ADHD. X-rays of the long bones for fractures (Choice D) help assess for a history of child abuse. This patient has symptoms of ADHD, which can explain his history of frequent injuries. EEG (Choice E) helps assess for seizures. This patient's history of injuries is more likely due to impulse dysregulation in the setting of ADHD.</p>", "39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Delusional disorder is a psychotic disorder that features the presence of one or more delusions for 1 month or longer with no other psychotic or mood symptoms. Treatment commonly includes antipsychotic medication and psychotherapy.</p><p>Delusional disorder is a psychotic disorder that features the presence of one or more delusions for 1 month or longer with no other psychotic symptoms (eg, hallucinations, disorganized thinking, disorganized behaviors other than behaviors related to the delusion) or mood symptoms. Patients lack other signs of a severe thought disorder such as cognitive impairment (as evidenced by this patient's appropriately abstract interpretation of a proverb) and affect that is incongruent with the topic of conversation. Persecutory delusions (as in this patient) and somatic delusions are common types of delusions. Many patients will only present for care at the insistence of other people given their lack of insight into the psychotic nature of their concern. Treatment typically includes antipsychotic medication and psychotherapy with the goal of improving the patient's coping with the delusion and gradually increasing insight into the delusion. The prognosis is guarded.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Bipolar disorder (Choice A) is characterized by discrete episodes of depression and mania, which can sometimes be accompanied by psychotic symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. This patient does not report mood symptoms (eg, depressed or euphoric mood, increased or decreased sleep). Borderline personality disorder (Choice B) is a cluster B personality disorder, the emotional or dramatic cluster, that features emotional dysregulation, an unstable sense of self, and tumultuous relationships. When stressed, patients with borderline personality disorder can demonstrate paranoid thinking. However, this patient's delusion has persisted independent of her mental state, which is more consistent with delusional disorder. Paranoid personality disorder (Choice D) is a cluster A personality disorder, the odd or eccentric cluster, characterized by a pervasive mistrust of others and viewing others as possessing malicious intent. Personality disorders typically begin in young adulthood. This patient's psychotic symptoms developed in middle age, which is inconsistent with a personality disorder. Schizophrenia (Choice E) is a chronic psychotic disorder characterized by delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, and negative symptoms (eg, alogia, apathy, flat affect). This patient demonstrates an isolated delusion with no disorganization of speech/thinking, hallucinations, or negative symptoms. She also lacks cognitive impairment, a common finding in schizophrenia.</p>", "40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) commonly cause sexual dysfunction within all three phases of sexual response (libido, sexual arousal, and orgasm). If a patient's decreased libido is persistent and distressing, initial management commonly involves decreasing the SSRI dose.</p><p>Increased serotonin likely explains this patient's sexual dysfunction. Fluoxetine is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) that increases synaptic serotonin activity and improves mood in patients with major depressive disorder. SSRIs commonly cause sexual dysfunction within all three phases of sexual response (libido, sexual arousal, and orgasm). If a patient's decreased libido is persistent and distressing, initial management commonly involves decreasing the SSRI dose, especially if depression has been in sustained remission. If this is ineffective, then the physician can consider discontinuing fluoxetine since this patient's depression has been in sustained remission, and she has only experienced one depressive episode. If the patient prefers to stay on antidepressant medication to prevent future depressive episodes, then adding bupropion or switching to an antidepressant with a lower risk for sexual dysfunction (eg, bupropion, mirtazapine) would be reasonable options.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Initiating propranolol (Choice B) is unlikely to help sexual dysfunction. As well, propranolol is not helpful for major depressive disorder. Initiating sildenafil (Choice C) is unlikely to be helpful. Sildenafil can be helpful in treating antidepressant-induced arousal problems and anorgasmia but has not been shown to affect libido. Recommending weekend drug holidays (Choice D) is not a commonly used strategy for addressing sexual dysfunction and would interfere with the antidepressant effect of fluoxetine. Replacing fluoxetine with citalopram (Choice E) is unlikely to resolve this patient's SSRI-induced sexual dysfunction, as citalopram carries a similar risk for sexual dysfunction compared with fluoxetine.</p>", "41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Panic disorder is an anxiety disorder characterized by recurrent panic attacks that are unexpected and associated with worry about future panic attacks or avoidance of panic attack triggers. Panic disorder is postulated to develop because of catastrophic appraisals of internal body states. As such, some studies have shown that patients with panic disorder commonly experience panic attacks in response to lactate infusions.</p><p>Panic attacks feature acute fear or anxiety that peaks within minutes and is associated with four additional physical symptoms of sympathetic hyperarousal or associated mental states. These additional symptoms may include heart palpitations or tachycardia, shortness of breath, chest pain, dizziness, the sensation of choking, gastrointestinal distress, paresthesia, sweating, chills, trembling, derealization, the fear of dying, and/or the fear of losing control. Panic disorder is an anxiety disorder characterized by recurrent panic attacks that are unexpected and associated with worry about future panic attacks or avoidance of panic attack triggers. The pathogenesis involves genetic factors, an anxious temperament, and stressful life events leading to catastrophic appraisals of internal body states such as normal sympathetic activity. Lactate infusions recreate a state of bodily stress. It has been found that patients with panic disorder experience panic attacks in response to lactate at an increased rate compared with the general population. However, there is an absence of standardized criteria for lactate-induced panic attacks, so the diagnostic utility of this test is unclear. Treatment of panic disorder typically includes antidepressant medication and/or cognitive behavioral therapy with a focus on decreasing patients’ catastrophic appraisal of bodily sensations of sympathetic hyperarousal and increasing patients’ ability to cope with panic attack sensations.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Abnormal eye tracking (Choice A) is found in patients with schizophrenia. Patients with panic disorder do not demonstrate abnormal eye tracking. Delayed REM sleep on polysomnography (Choice B) can be found in sleep apnea and periodic limb movement disorder. Certain medications such as tricyclic antidepressants, monoamine oxidase inhibitors, amphetamine, and barbiturates as well as alcohol also delay the REM sleep phase. No known association exists between delayed REM sleep and panic disorder. Enlarged third and lateral ventricles on CT scan of the head (Choice C) occurs as a result of stenosis of the aqueduct of Sylvius (cerebral aqueduct), the most common cause of congenital hydrocephalus. This would be atypical in a previously healthy young adult and would not explain her array of symptoms. Nonsuppression of cortisol after administration of 1 mg of dexamethasone (Choice E) is consistent with Cushing syndrome, not panic disorder.</p>", "42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: REM sleep behavior disorder features absent muscle atonia during REM sleep. Patients typically act out their dreams in a coordinated though sometimes violent manner. REM sleep behavior disorder typically occurs in adult males and can be idiopathic or related to underlying a-synuclein deposition (eg, Parkinson disease, multiple system atrophy, or Lewy body dementia).</p><p>In typical REM sleep, several neural circuits terminate on spinal cord motor neurons to cause sleep atonia. This muscle atonia is lost in REM sleep behavior disorder, in which patients typically violently act out their dreams. They typically, but not always, remember the dreams. REM sleep behavior disorder most commonly occurs in adult males and can be idiopathic or related to underlying a-synuclein deposition (eg, Parkinson disease, multiple system atrophy, or Lewy body dementia). Polysomnography showing absent atonia during REM sleep confirms the diagnosis. Treatment includes creating a safe sleep environment and, if the behavior is severe, initiating melatonin or clonazepam. Patients with idiopathic REM sleep behavior disorder should be closely monitored due to the high rate of developing a neurodegenerative disease.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Antisocial personality disorder (Choice A), a cluster B personality disorder, the emotional or dramatic cluster, is characterized by a pervasive violation of others’ rights, aggression, and a hostile and manipulative attitude toward others. This patient is in REM sleep during these episodes and is therefore not demonstrating a conscious violation of others’ rights or a manipulative attitude. Complex partial seizures (Choice B) are characterized by repetitive movements during sleep with minimal memory of the event. This patient demonstrates coordinated sequences of movements rather than repetitive movements. Nightmare disorder (Choice C) describes the presence of recurrent disturbing dreams. This patient makes no report of disturbing dream activity though such can be inferred from his behavior. While his dreams may be nightmares, the absence of sleep atonia makes REM sleep behavior disorder more likely. Nocturnal panic attacks (Choice D) occur during the night and wake up the patient. This patient is still asleep while acting out his dreams.</p>", "43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Oppositional defiant disorder manifests as a persistent pattern of angry/irritable mood, defiant behavior, and vindictiveness that causes social, academic, or occupational dysfunction. Treatment commonly includes parent management training.</p><p>Oppositional defiant disorder is a childhood disruptive behavior disorder that manifests as a persistent pattern of angry/irritable mood, defiant behavior (eg, arguing with or defying authority figures, blaming others for their own mistakes), and vindictiveness. These behaviors cause dysfunction in the patient's immediate social context (most commonly with family members) or lead to educational or occupational dysfunction. This patient demonstrates defiant behavior toward his parents, teachers, and the physician. Oppositional defiant disorder commonly arises from a combination of genetic factors, an irritable temperament, and, sometimes, childhood neglect or a harsh parenting style. Comorbid attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), learning disorders, anxiety disorders, and substance use disorders are common. Management is multimodal and includes parent management training (teaching parents to reinforce positive and punish negative behaviors), school-based interventions, and individual anger management therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Insight-oriented psychotherapy (Choice B) and psychoanalytic psychotherapy (Choice C) both attempt to uncover unconscious thoughts and feelings that drive present-day behaviors and relationships. Young children are unlikely to have the reflective capacity to gain insight into their unconscious motivators. Lorazepam therapy (Choice D) can be used to treat comorbid anxiety. This patient does not demonstrate anxiety symptoms. Methylphenidate therapy (Choice E) is used to treat comorbid ADHD. This patient does not demonstrate ADHD symptoms.</p>", "44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Premature ejaculation, a type of sexual dysfunction, is commonly associated with psychological factors such as performance anxiety and post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, which can delay ejaculation, are the first-line pharmacological treatment for premature ejaculation and PTSD.</p><p>This patient demonstrates premature ejaculation, a type of sexual dysfunction. Psychological factors such as performance anxiety, marital conflict, or post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD, as in this patient) can exert a strong influence on sexual function. In males, anxiety can lead to decreased libido, decreased ability to obtain or maintain an erection, premature ejaculation, or decreased ability to ejaculate. Treatment strategies for sexual dysfunction include cognitive behavioral therapy, mindfulness meditation training, or pharmacotherapy. First-line pharmacotherapy for premature ejaculation is selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) therapy, particularly paroxetine. SSRIs have an unclear mechanism for delaying ejaculation that likely involves descending serotonin pathways. Patients with PTSD experience intrusive thoughts/memories (eg, nightmares, flashbacks), dysregulation of arousal (eg, irritability, angry outbursts, restlessness, easy startle), avoidance of reminders (eg, staying in the home), and negative mood/thoughts (eg, psychomotor retardation, feelings of detachment). SSRIs are also a first-line treatment for PTSD.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Bupropion (Choice A) is an antidepressant that increases synaptic norepinephrine and dopamine. Bupropion has a small amount of evidence for the treatment of premature ejaculation, but SSRIs have more evidence for their effectiveness. As well, bupropion has no proven efficacy for PTSD. Buspirone (Choice B) is a serotonin modulator useful for generalized anxiety disorder. It has no use for premature ejaculation. Lorazepam (Choice C) is a short-acting benzodiazepine. It has been found to worsen PTSD outcomes, likely by promoting avoidance of trauma-related emotions and thus preventing habituation of these emotions. Mirtazapine (Choice D), an atypical antidepressant medication, is a selective ag-receptor antagonist and serotonin modulator. It has not proven efficacious for premature ejaculation.</p>", "45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder that features acute episodes of mania and may include episodes of depression. Acute mania typically manifests with an irritable or euphoric mood, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility, and grandiose thinking. Some patients demonstrate associated psychotic symptoms.</p><p>This patient is experiencing an episode of acute mania and can therefore be diagnosed with bipolar disorder. Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder that features acute episodes of mania and may include episodes of depression. Acute mania typically manifests with an irritable or euphoric mood, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility, and grandiose thinking. Some patients demonstrate associated psychotic symptoms (eg, grandiose delusions as in this patient, hallucinations). Acute mania can severely disrupt patients’ daily function and endanger their safety, as in this patient. Management of acute mania typically requires mood stabilizing therapy (eg, mood stabilizers or mood stabilizing atypical antipsychotics). patient. Management of acute mania typically requires mood stabilizing therapy (eg, mood stabilizers or mood stabilizing atypical antipsychotics).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Brief psychotic disorder (Choice B) is characterized by at least one acute psychotic symptom (eg, delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior) lasting less than 1 month. This patient's psychotic symptoms appear to be secondary to mania. Delirium, alcohol withdrawal (Choice C), or delirium tremens, refers to an alcohol withdrawal that typically presents with autonomic instability (eg, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypertension, hyperthermia), tremors, anxiety, hallucinations, confusion, delirium, and ataxia, and may result in convulsions, coma, and death. This patient has normal vital signs and is fully oriented. Delusional disorder (Choice D) presents with fixed delusions in the absence of other psychiatric symptoms, such as hallucinations, disorganization, depression, or mania. Malingering (Choice E) refers to consciously producing or falsifying symptoms for a conscious purpose such as obtaining disability payments or release from jail. It is unlikely that someone would volitionally walk outside in the cold to the point of inducing frostbite for secondary gain. As well, this patient has no clear source of secondary gain. Schizoid personality disorder (Choice F), a cluster A personality disorder, manifests as extreme social detachment and a cold, restricted affect. These patients typically disengage from society. This patient is playful and talkative. Schizophrenia (Choice G) presents with at least two of the following five symptoms: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, or negative symptoms (eg, flat affect, apathy, alogia). This patient does not demonstrate negative symptoms, instead presenting with acute manic symptoms consistent with bipolar disorder.</p>", "46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute stress disorder presents similarly to post-traumatic stress disorder, with symptoms of intrusive thoughts, hyperarousal, avoidance, and/or negative mood/thoughts within 1 month of a traumatic event. Trauma-focused cognitive behavioral therapy may be helpful.</p><p>Acute stress disorder (ASD) presents similarly to post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) with intrusive thoughts/memories (eg, nightmares, flashbacks), dysregulation of arousal (eg, nervousness, insomnia, easy startle, decreased concentration), avoidance of reminders, and negative mood/thoughts (eg, psychomotor retardation, feelings of detachment) in response to violence or threatened violence. Like in PTSD, patients can also experience dissociation, the mind’s attempt to decrease conscious awareness of painful affects by decreasing its awareness of reality (as in this patient's depersonalization and derealization). However, ASD presents within 1 month of a traumatic event, whereas PTSD lasts more than 1 month beyond the event. ASD typically lasts between 3 days to 1 month. Pharmacotherapy is typically not indicated, but trauma-focused cognitive behavioral therapy may be helpful. In this case, providing normalization about the range of reactions to trauma and education about when to seek psychiatric help will be most helpful.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Adjustment disorder with mixed anxiety and depressed mood (Choice B) refers to the onset of disproportionate distress, with associated anxiety and depressive symptoms, within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. Intrusive thoughts/memories and arousal dysregulation are specific to ASD/PTSD. Brief psychotic disorder (Choice C) is characterized by at least one acute psychotic symptom (eg, delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior) lasting less than 1 month. This patient is experiencing trauma-induced dissociation, not psychotic symptoms. Depersonalization disorder (Choice D) is a type of dissociative disorder involving detachment from the self. The detachment must cause clinically significant distress or dysfunction. This patient's depersonalization does not clearly cause daily dysfunction and can be conceptualized as part of ASD given her other trauma-related symptoms. Post-traumatic stress disorder (Choice E) refers to symptoms of intrusive thoughts/memories of trauma, hyperarousal, avoidance of trauma reminders, and/or negative mood/thoughts resulting from a traumatic event such as a life-threatening motor vehicle collision and lasting more than 1 month. This patient's symptoms have lasted 1 week.</p>", "47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Methamphetamine is a stimulant that causes euphoria and increases sympathetic tone, leading to tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, pupillary dilation, and restlessness. Due to increased synaptic dopamine, agitation, hallucinations, and paranoia can occur.</p><p>This patient is likely intoxicated with methamphetamine. Amphetamines are central nervous system stimulants that cause euphoria and increase sympathetic tone due to increased catecholamine concentration, leading to tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, pupillary dilation, diaphoresis, agitation, and restlessness. Appetite suppression and weight loss are common. Patients with chronic methamphetamine use disorder often have periodontal disease such as stained teeth, cavities, or missing teeth. Chronic methamphetamine use can also lead to methamphetamine-induced psychotic disorder, which features chronic delusions, paranoia, and hallucinations. Tactile or visual hallucinations of parasitosis may occur.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and I. Alcohol intoxication (Choice A) can cause slurred speech, emotional lability, behavioral disinhibition, ataxia, and memory lapses (like benzodiazepine intoxication). In severe intoxication, stupor and respiratory depression can occur. Alcohol withdrawal (Choice B) causes anxiety, tremors, diaphoresis, nausea and vomiting, and hallucinations. Complicated alcohol withdrawal refers to a severe, life-threatening type of withdrawal that includes seizures or delirium tremens. Bipolar disorder (Choice C) is characterized by discrete episodes of depression and mania, which can sometimes be accompanied by psychotic symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. Tachycardia and excoriations would be uncommon, and primary psychiatric disorders are more frequently associated with auditory, rather than visual, hallucinations. Delusional disorder (Choice D) features one or more delusions for 1 month or longer with no other psychotic symptoms. This patient does not meet the duration criterion for delusional disorder and demonstrates disorganized behavior and vital sign abnormalities more consistent with methamphetamine intoxication. Generalized anxiety disorder (Choice E) features excessive and persistent worrying that may be associated with sympathetic hyperactivity. It would be unlikely to cause tactile or visual hallucinations and paranoia. Marijuana intoxication (Choice F) causes euphoria, paranoia, hallucinations, cognitive slowing, conjunctival injection, and dilated pupils. Patients withdrawing from marijuana (Choice G) experience irritability, anxiety, depression, insomnia, restlessness, and decreased appetite. Methamphetamine withdrawal (Choice I) typically leads to sedation, fatigue, and increased appetite.</p>", "48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Antisocial personality disorder is a cluster B personality disorder, the emotional or dramatic cluster, that is postulated to arise from deficient serotonin signaling and consequent dysphoria. This dysphoric affect combined with a lack of empathy leads to pervasive violations of others’ rights, aggression, and a hostile and manipulative attitude toward others.</p><p>Antisocial personality disorder (ASPD) is a cluster B personality disorder, the emotional or dramatic cluster, that arises from a deficit in empathy, resulting in pervasive violations of others’ rights, aggression, and a hostile and manipulative attitude toward others (as in this patient's theft of her boyfriend's television set and her son's methylphenidate). Like other cluster B personality disorders, patients with ASPD struggle to regulate their emotions in that their frustration tolerance is low (as manifested in this patient's suicidality in the setting of a relatively minor stressor). They frequently behave impulsively. It is postulated that genetic polymorphisms in the serotonin transporter gene lead to deficient serotonin signaling and consequent dysphoria. Some studies have identified a higher-than-average rate of suicide among patients with ASPD, which may be related to their negative emotionality, hostility, and low restraint. These patients may also be chronically bored and hence sensation seeking, leading to a high rate of substance use (as manifested in this patient's rewarding feeling after taking methylphenidate) and gambling. These personality traits lead to deficits in personal (extreme egocentrism) and interpersonal (empathy, intimacy) functioning. Personality disorders are persistently maladaptive ways of relating to the self and to society that typically appear by early adulthood.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Acute intermittent porphyria (Choice A) presents after an acute metabolic stress (eg, starvation, alcohol ingestion) with psychosis (eg, hallucinations, delusions), abdominal pain, and burgundy-colored urine. This patient's aggression is caused by an absence of empathy, not psychosis. Adjustment disorder with depressed mood (Choice B) refers to the onset of disproportionate distress, with predominant depressive symptoms, within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. This patient demonstrates a chronic and pervasive pattern of behavior that precedes the stressor of the argument. Paranoid personality disorder (Choice D) is a cluster A personality disorder, the odd or eccentric cluster, characterized by a pervasive mistrust of others and viewing others as possessing malicious intent. This patient is unable to empathize with others but does not overtly mistrust others. As well, pervasive aggression is more consistent with ASPD. Premenstrual dysphoric disorder (Choice E) consists of several affective and somatic symptoms occurring in the week before menses and resolving with the onset of menses. This patient does not demonstrate cyclicity of her symptoms. As well, her suicidality is occurring in the week after her menses. Substance-induced mood disorder (Choice F) refers to depression symptoms secondary to substance use. This patient has taken her son's methylphenidate in the past but is not currently using substances. Temporal lobe epilepsy (Choice G) presents with recurrent episodes of muscle spasms, sensory changes, or emotional/behavioral changes in the form of epileptic activity (seizures). This patient's emotional and behavioral changes are persistent and pervasive rather than episodic.</p>", "49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Normal-pressure hydrocephalus refers to a type of communicating (nonobstructive) hydrocephalus in which the ventricles are enlarged, leading to damage of the white matter tracts connecting the frontal lobes and basal ganglia. Patients classically present with insidious-onset dementia, urinary incontinence, and gait dysfunction early in the disease course. Many patients improve with lumbar puncture and may benefit from a long-term ventricular shunt.</p><p>Normal-pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) refers to a type of communicating (nonobstructive) hydrocephalus in which the ventricles are enlarged, leading to damage of the white matter tracts connecting the frontal lobes and basal ganglia. NPH typically presents in older patients with insidious onset of dementia (eg, frontal executive dysfunction), gait apraxia, and urinary incontinence. The gait apraxia may manifest as a wide-based or magnetic gait. The central vestibular tract, a white matter tract located in the periventricular area, may also be affected by ventriculomegaly, leading to this patient's postural instability. Signs of increased intracranial pressure (eg, papilledema, postural headaches) are typically absent. Many cases of NPH are idiopathic though some are secondary to a defect in cerebrospinal fluid drainage from the ventricles due to scarring or obstruction of the ventricular lining from hemorrhage or meningitis. The diagnosis is primarily clinical, although S the white matter tracts connecting the frontal lobes and basal ganglia. NPH typically presents in older patients with insidious onset of dementia (eg, frontal executive ~ dysfunction), gait apraxia, and urinary incontinence. The gait apraxia may manifest as a wide-based or magnetic gait. The central vestibular tract, a white matter tract located in the periventricular area, may also be affected by ventriculomegaly, leading to this patient's postural instability. Signs of increased intracranial pressure (eg, papilledema, postural headaches) are typically absent. Many cases of NPH are idiopathic though some are secondary to a defect in cerebrospinal fluid drainage from the ventricles due to scarring or obstruction of the ventricular lining from hemorrhage or meningitis. The diagnosis is primarily clinical, although ventriculomegaly on brain imaging is suggestive. Patients may improve rapidly in response to drainage of cerebrospinal fluid during a lumbar puncture (known as a positive lumbar tap test), which supports the diagnosis. Management commonly includes ventricular shunting for patients with a positive lumbar tap test.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Dementia, vascular type (Choice A), also known as multi-infarct dementia, presents with a stepwise decline in cognitive performance over several years and focal neurologic deficits. It occurs as a result of cumulative damage from many small ischemic strokes. X-rays often show evidence of microvascular disease. The gradual onset of this patient's decline as well as his gait changes and urinary symptoms are more suggestive of NPH. Frontotemporal dementia (Choice B), also called Pick disease, is a neurodegenerative disorder characterized by atrophy of the frontal and temporal lobes, causing deficits in social behavior or personality along with dementia. The onset of frontotemporal dementia is typically in the sixth decade of life. This patient does not demonstrate behavioral changes and is older than the typical demographic. Additionally, his gait and urinary symptoms are better explained by NPH. Parkinson disease (Choice D) is a neurologic syndrome that features bradykinesia, resting tremor, rigidity (frequently associated with cogwheel phenomenon), and postural instability. Dementia is demonstrated late in the disease course, and this patient lacks most of the cardinal motor symptoms of Parkinson disease. Pseudodementia secondary to major depressive disorder (Choice E) can be observed in older patients, but this patient does not demonstrate adequate criteria needed for the diagnosis of depression, such as depressed mood, anhedonia, guilt, loss of energy, poor concentration, poor appetite, psychomotor retardation, or suicidal ideation.</p>", "50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Symptoms of major depressive disorder include 2 or more weeks of depressed mood (or irritable mood in pediatric patients), anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor slowing, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). Symptoms disrupt everyday functioning (eg, declining grades and school avoidance in pediatric patients, home dysfunction, social withdrawal).</p><p>This patient meets criteria for major depressive disorder (MDD). MDD is characterized by 2 or more weeks of at least five of the following symptoms: depressed mood (or irritable mood in pediatric patients), anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor slowing, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). Patients commonly demonstrate negativistic thinking about themselves, others, and/or the world. Symptoms disrupt everyday functioning (eg, declining grades and school avoidance in pediatric patients, home dysfunction, social withdrawal). Additional somatic symptoms such as stomachaches are common. Prominent mental status examination findings commonly include a depressed affect with a decreased affective range and latent and sparse speech. Patients with MDD should be carefully assessed for suicidal ideation. The most effective treatment for severe MDD (as in this patient) includes the combination of psychotherapy and antidepressant medication.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Adjustment disorder (Choice A) refers to the onset of disproportionate distress within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. It is commonly associated with depressive symptoms that do not meet the diagnostic criteria for MDD. This patient meets the diagnostic criteria of MDD. Borderline personality disorder (Choice B) is a cluster B personality disorder, the emotional or dramatic cluster, that features emotional dysregulation, an unstable sense of self, and tumultuous relationships. Histrionic personality disorder (Choice D), a cluster B personality disorder, is characterized by theatrical, superficial expressions of emotion that unconsciously serve to garner attention from others to fulfill emotional needs. This patient is experiencing a consistently depressed mood, not a dysregulated/fluctuating mood or superficial emotions. As well, an official personality disorder diagnosis requires that the patient be 18 years old or older. Dissociative identity disorder (Choice C), formerly known as multiple personality disorder, refers to patients transitioning between several identities and is frequently related to childhood trauma. This consistently depressed patient does not demonstrate multiple identities. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (Choice F) is an anxiety disorder that manifests with recurrent, intrusive urges or thoughts (obsessions) that the patient tries to 7 relieve or neutralize by repetitive rituals (compulsions). Though this patient worries, he does not demonstrate compulsions. As well, his most prominent symptoms are depressive rather than anxious. Psychoactive substance use disorder (Choice G) must meet at least two criteria, including an inability to cut back on a psychoactive substance, using more than expected, substances cravings, and persistent substance use despite negative social consequences. This patient does not demonstrate these symptoms. In somatic symptom disorder (Choice H), the patient is excessively preoccupied with one or more somatic symptoms such that these symptoms disrupt daily life. This patient is not preoccupied with his stomachaches. Depressive symptoms most likely explain his school avoidance.</p>"};
